const STORIES = [{"id": "friendships-screen", "title": "The Screenshot", "category": "Friendships", "teaser": "A private joke becomes a public problem. Staying quiet would be easier. Until it isn't.", "skill": "Setting boundaries · Speaking up · Repairing trust", "scenes": [{"title": "Seen by everyone", "text": ["After the basketball game, Nia tried finishing homework while her group chat flashed. Someone posted Eli asleep on the bus. Nia sent a laughing emoji before reading the cruel caption.", "Then her phone buzzed with a private message from Eli: ‘You knew I was exhausted from taking care of my little brother. Why did you laugh?’ Nia opened the chat again. Her emoji was sitting beneath a joke about Eli being lazy. She hadn't written the joke. She had still helped it land.", "A new screenshot appeared in another group. The joke was traveling. Tomorrow, Nia would have to walk into school beside the people in that picture and the people laughing at it."]}, {"title": "The first reply", "text": ["Nia typed ‘I didn't mean it like that’ and erased it. She wanted Eli to understand her intention, but his message was about what happened, not what she intended. Across the room, her unfinished outline waited.", "The group chat asked where she'd gone. ‘Don't get serious,’ someone wrote. Nia didn't want one bad moment to become a fight with everybody. She also didn't want Eli to carry it alone."], "question": "What should Nia do first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Message Eli privately, acknowledge the emoji, and ask what support would help.", "consequence": "Eli replies after a long pause. ‘Just don't act like it's nothing.’ Nia has opened a conversation, but a private apology will not stop a public post. She realizes repair may need more than one step."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the group to remove the screenshot before answering Eli.", "consequence": "Two people delete it. One argues that everyone is too sensitive. Nia stops some sharing, but Eli still doesn't know whether she understands why he was hurt. She needs to speak to him directly too."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Put the phone down briefly so she can calm down and choose her words.", "consequence": "Ten minutes gives Nia room to think instead of firing back. The chat keeps moving, though. A pause can help her respond thoughtfully; it cannot become a plan to avoid responding altogether."}]}, {"title": "Don't make it weird", "text": ["At lunch, Nia's friend Cam slides into the next chair. ‘You made everybody uncomfortable last night.’ Nia looks past Cam toward Eli, who is eating with another group. She doesn't want to perform an argument for the cafeteria.", "‘I still want to hang out with y'all,’ she says. Cam shrugs. ‘Then stop acting like our principal.’ Nia feels the familiar pull to laugh and move on. Belonging matters to her. So does being able to disagree without losing her place."], "question": "How can Nia express a boundary without turning the conversation into an insult?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say, ‘I'm still your friend. I'm not joining jokes that expose someone's private life.’", "consequence": "Cam rolls their eyes, then asks what counts as private. Nia names the screenshot and family details rather than attacking Cam's character. Agreement isn't immediate, but her boundary is clear and specific."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask to talk away from the lunch table, where neither has an audience.", "consequence": "Outside the cafeteria, Cam admits being afraid the whole group will split. Nia can take that concern seriously without withdrawing her own. Privacy lowers the temperature; the boundary still needs to be said."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say she needs space today and will talk when they can both listen.", "consequence": "Nia leaves with her lunch instead of trading sharp remarks. Cam doesn't follow. The space protects her from an escalating exchange, but she plans a later conversation so silence doesn't have to speak for her."}]}, {"title": "The bigger choice", "text": ["By last period, another account has posted the image. This time the caption names Eli's family. Nia has already asked one person to stop. Now she wonders whether asking again is enough.", "Eli says, ‘I don't want everybody called into the office because of me.’ Nia hears his wish for control. She also sees that the sharing has grown beyond their circle. She can support him without promising secrecy she might not be able to keep."], "question": "What is a useful next step?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Offer to go with Eli to a trusted adult and let him explain what help he wants.", "consequence": "Eli agrees to talk with the counselor privately. Nia stays beside him but lets him lead. The counselor explains possible steps instead of promising an instant fix. Eli gets support without Nia taking over his story."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Help Eli save evidence and report the post, then discuss adult support if it continues.", "consequence": "They report rather than reshare the image. The post may not disappear immediately. They choose a trusted adult to contact if harassment continues or they feel unsafe, so the plan has a next step beyond waiting."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask the counselor privately how to help without repeating every personal detail.", "consequence": "Nia gets guidance, but learns that staff may need to act on safety concerns. She tells Eli what she asked rather than hiding it. Getting advice can help, especially when she is honest about its limits."}]}, {"title": "A different group chat", "text": ["That evening, the chat is quieter. There is no dramatic message declaring everyone fixed. Cam sends a basketball clip. Eli sends Nia a single sentence: ‘Thanks for not making me explain everything twice.’ Some trust will take longer.", "Nia returns to her history outline. She hasn't chosen between having friends and having boundaries. She is learning that friendship has to leave room for both. Before sending her next message, she reads it once as herself and once as the person receiving it.", "Reflection: Which choice protected someone's privacy? What sentence could you use when friends pressure you to join something you don't want to do? Explain how you could support a friend without speaking over them."]}]}, {"id": "summer-two-plans", "title": "Two Summers", "category": "Summer plans", "teaser": "The trip is real. So is the job offer. One missing detail could cost both.", "skill": "Planning ahead · Asking questions · Communicating commitments", "scenes": [{"title": "The offer", "text": ["Lakeside Recreation offered Diego a paid summer position. Training began the first Monday in June.", "On the kitchen counter sat a family calendar with that same week circled. His aunt had rented a cabin for a trip planned months ago. Diego had told his cousins he would be there. He had not mentioned the trip during the interview because nobody asked.", "His cousin sent a countdown to the trip. Then the recreation director requested confirmation by tomorrow. Two promises, one date."]}, {"title": "Before saying yes", "text": ["Diego wants to accept before the position goes to somebody else. He also doesn't know whether every training day is required or whether missing it means losing the job. Guessing feels faster than admitting he needs information.", "His friend says, ‘Just say yes. You can deal with the trip later.’ Diego opens a reply. The cursor blinks beneath the director's name. He needs an answer that is honest without deciding the whole summer in one sentence."], "question": "How should Diego respond to the offer?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain the existing trip and ask which training dates are required before confirming.", "consequence": "The director appreciates knowing early. She says safety training is mandatory and promises to check one alternative session. Diego has not secured an exception, but he now has real information instead of a hopeful guess."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for the training schedule and a brief call before the acceptance deadline.", "consequence": "The schedule arrives that afternoon. Diego can see the conflict clearly. The call will only help if he names it honestly, so he writes down the trip dates and the question he needs answered."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask whether the confirmation deadline can be extended one day while he checks a family commitment.", "consequence": "The director can give him only until noon, not another full day. Asking is allowed; getting the exact answer he wants isn't guaranteed. Diego uses the extra time to collect facts rather than postponing the conversation."}]}, {"title": "The kitchen calendar", "text": ["At home, Diego's aunt says the cabin is already paid for. His cousin says, ‘So a summer job matters more than us?’ Diego wants to answer with the same sharpness. Instead, he notices that everyone is talking about what they might lose.", "The job matters because Diego wants experience and savings. The trip matters because the family rarely gets time together. He doesn't need to pretend one is worthless to explain why the other matters. He does need to say what he can actually commit to."], "question": "What could Diego say to his family?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain why the job matters, acknowledge the trip costs, and ask to compare options together.", "consequence": "His aunt is still disappointed, but she starts discussing dates instead of motives. Diego hears about costs he hadn't considered. The conversation becomes a problem they can examine, not a contest about who cares more."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask to write out each option and its costs before anyone decides.", "consequence": "The list reveals a possible shorter trip, but transportation would cost extra. A written plan makes the tradeoffs visible. Diego still needs to listen to feelings; a spreadsheet alone cannot settle a family disappointment."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask for a short pause, then return with the confirmed training information.", "consequence": "Diego avoids making an angry promise. He tells them exactly when he will come back to talk. When he returns, he brings facts and listens instead of using the pause to disappear from the decision."}]}, {"title": "One workable option", "text": ["The director calls back. There is a safety session the following week, but the first work shift would still be missed. She can offer a later start with fewer hours, if Diego confirms today. It is not the perfect solution he hoped for.", "Diego checks the numbers. Fewer hours mean slower savings. His aunt can take him on the original trip. Another option is to leave the cabin early with a relative, but that would require a dependable ride and clear dates. He has enough information now to make a choice he can explain."], "question": "How should Diego make the final commitment?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Choose the later start, acknowledge the reduced hours, and confirm the exact training date in writing.", "consequence": "The director confirms the revised dates. Diego keeps the trip and adjusts his savings goal. He has not escaped a tradeoff, but he has made a realistic promise that the family and employer can both understand."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Choose to leave the trip early, but confirm the ride before committing to the employer.", "consequence": "The relative agrees to the ride and the arrival time works. Diego then confirms his work schedule. Checking transportation first prevents a good intention from becoming a missed shift. He also tells his cousins when he will leave."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decline this position respectfully and ask about future openings while keeping the trip commitment.", "consequence": "The director thanks him for a clear answer. There is no guarantee of another opening, so Diego begins a new job search. Choosing family time can be valid; he owns the financial consequence rather than blaming the employer."}]}, {"title": "A calendar he can trust", "text": ["Diego adds the final dates to the calendar and sends the same plan to everyone affected. His summer is not as simple as the countdown made it look. It is better organized, though, and he doesn't have to keep track of two different stories about where he'll be.", "He writes three questions in the notes on his phone: What is required? What can change? What am I promising? The next time an opportunity arrives, he wants excitement and information in the same conversation.", "Reflection: Which tradeoff would matter most to you? Explain why asking a question before accepting can show responsibility. Write a short message you could send when two commitments conflict."]}]}, {"id": "christmas-quiet-seat", "title": "A Seat at the Table", "category": "Christmas break", "teaser": "Everyone has plans for Lena's holiday. She has a plan too. Saying it out loud changes the evening.", "skill": "Expressing needs · Negotiating responsibilities · Respectful boundaries", "scenes": [{"title": "The list on the fridge", "text": ["On Christmas break, Lena found four jobs beside her name on the refrigerator: babysit, wrap gifts, prepare dinner, and clean. She had only agreed to help with dinner.", "She liked her cousins. She also had a training-program application due tomorrow. With the house this loud, she kept rereading the same question. Her aunt passed with groceries. “You’re home all week anyway,” she said.", "Lena wanted a say in her time, without seeming ungrateful or announcing private plans to everyone. Her application was due tomorrow, quiet house or not."]}, {"title": "An actual conversation", "text": ["Her aunt asks why Lena looks upset. ‘Nothing,’ Lena nearly says. It would be easy to stay quiet now and angry later. Instead, she thinks about what she needs: ninety quiet minutes today and a clear agreement about which tasks are hers.", "She can explain that without proving she is busier than everyone else. Her aunt is carrying a lot too. The question is how they can make a plan that includes both of them."], "question": "How can Lena begin?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask for a private minute and explain the deadline, quiet time needed, and tasks she can do.", "consequence": "Her aunt hadn't known about the application. She agrees to talk away from the cousins. Lena offers specific help instead of a vague refusal, and her aunt can respond to an actual request rather than trying to read her expression."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Write a proposed schedule and ask her aunt to review it with her.", "consequence": "The written plan makes the ninety-minute gap clear. Her aunt points out that dinner preparation overlaps. They move one chore instead of treating the whole plan as impossible. Writing helps Lena organize her words before the conversation."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say she is overwhelmed and ask to choose her responsibilities rather than taking every task.", "consequence": "Her aunt asks which task is hardest to manage. Lena explains the noise and deadline instead of just saying ‘all of it.’ Naming a need opens the door, but she still needs to negotiate specific responsibilities and times."}]}, {"title": "The joke at dinner", "text": ["Later, a relative announces, ‘Look who's too important to babysit now.’ A few people laugh. Lena feels her face warm. The comment turns a private request into entertainment, and she doesn't want to spend dinner defending herself.", "She could ignore one remark, respond briefly, or talk afterward. None of those choices requires pretending the joke felt good. What matters is choosing a response she can stand behind, rather than letting embarrassment decide for her."], "question": "How could Lena handle the comment?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say, ‘I helped today. I also needed time for my application. Please don't joke about that.’", "consequence": "The table goes quiet for a moment. Her aunt changes the subject, and dinner continues. Lena's words are direct without attacking the relative. An uncomfortable pause does not mean the boundary was wrong."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Let the moment pass, then privately explain that the joke embarrassed her.", "consequence": "Without an audience, the relative listens and says it was meant lightly. Lena explains that intent and impact can differ. A private conversation works here because she chooses it, not because she owes everyone a silent smile."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Take a brief break and ask a trusted relative to help her address it calmly.", "consequence": "In the hallway, Lena's cousin listens. Together they plan a short response if the joke returns. Support helps Lena steady herself; it doesn't mean the cousin should fight the whole conversation for her."}]}, {"title": "The plan changes", "text": ["The next morning, the younger cousins' ride is delayed. Lena's aunt asks her to watch them during the quiet hour they agreed on. The application is almost finished, but one section still needs concentration. Lena wants to help with the unexpected problem without losing her deadline.", "She checks what remains: fifteen minutes of writing, an attachment, and a final review. There may be a compromise, but it needs to protect enough time to finish. ‘I'll figure it out somehow’ is not a schedule."], "question": "What could Lena propose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Offer a shorter babysitting shift if another adult covers the final uninterrupted work period.", "consequence": "Her aunt finds someone to take over at the agreed time. Lena sets an alarm and hands off clearly. The compromise works because another person has actually agreed, not because Lena hopes help will appear."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Keep the protected hour and offer a different task after submitting the application.", "consequence": "Her aunt rearranges the morning and is briefly frustrated. Lena finishes, then follows through on the task she offered. Respecting a deadline can include disappointing someone without dismissing their needs."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask whether she can use a quieter room while a different adult supervises nearby.", "consequence": "They find a room with a door and confirm who is responsible for the cousins. Lena can work without pretending she can supervise and concentrate at once. A change of setting helps because the responsibilities are also clear."}]}, {"title": "Room for her plans", "text": ["When Lena submits the application, the house is still noisy. The holiday has not become a perfect movie. Her aunt asks how it went, and Lena explains the program while they carry dishes to the kitchen. This time, someone asks before adding her name to tomorrow's list.", "Lena has helped her family and made room for her own future. Neither happened automatically. She practiced saying what she needed, offering what she could, and keeping the promises she chose.", "Reflection: How can someone ask for time or space without insulting their family? Write one specific request Lena made. Then explain how she could respond if the first answer were no. You may discuss the fictional story without sharing private details about your own home."]}]}, {"id": "home-the-plan", "title": "Everyone Was Counting on Me", "category": "Home life", "teaser": "An interview, a younger brother, and a family plan nobody actually agreed on.", "skill": "Expressing needs and negotiating responsibilities", "scenes": [{"title": "A calendar with one missing detail", "text": ["Nia has spent a week preparing for a Saturday interview at a neighborhood recreation center. She has practiced her answers, borrowed a neat shirt, and checked the bus route twice. The position would give her summer experience and money she could save toward a car.", "On Friday evening, her aunt puts groceries on the kitchen counter. ‘Your brother's tournament starts at nine. I picked up an extra shift, so you'll need to take him.’ Nia's interview is at ten, across town. She mentioned it at dinner on Tuesday, but nobody put it on the family calendar.", "Her brother is listening from the doorway. Nia wants the interview. She also knows the extra shift matters to their household. She does not want the conversation to turn into a contest over whose plans count most."]}, {"title": "Not the answer she expected", "text": ["‘Can you move it?’ her aunt asks. ‘Everybody has to help.’ Nia hears that as ‘your plans don't matter,’ but she does not know whether that is what her aunt means. Her brother quietly asks whether he should tell his coach he cannot come.", "Nia has a few minutes before her aunt leaves again. She needs to explain the conflict, not defend her entire character. What should she try first?"], "question": "How can Nia make her need clear?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain the interview time and ask to solve the two schedules together.", "consequence": "Nia says, ‘I want to help, and I also need to keep this interview. It is at ten across town. Can we look at another ride for him?’ Her aunt pauses. She had thought the interview was next weekend. Correcting that detail does not produce a ride, but now they are solving the actual problem."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for five quiet minutes to show her aunt the interview message.", "consequence": "Her aunt initially says she is in a hurry. Nia asks for a specific time tonight rather than dropping the issue. They agree to talk at eight. Waiting gives Nia space to organize the details, but she will need to follow up before transportation options disappear."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Agree for now because she does not want her brother to feel responsible.", "consequence": "Her brother looks relieved, but Nia realizes she has promised two things she cannot do. She returns to the kitchen: ‘I answered too quickly. I can't cover the whole morning and make the interview.’ Changing her answer feels awkward, yet an honest correction is more useful than silently missing her opportunity."}]}, {"title": "Help has limits", "text": ["When they compare schedules, a possible plan emerges: a teammate's parent could take her brother to the tournament. Nobody has asked that parent yet. Nia can help him pack tonight and be home after her interview, but she cannot be in two places at once.", "‘Then you handle all of it,’ her aunt says, tired. Nia wants to contribute without becoming responsible for getting every adult to agree."], "question": "What responsibility can Nia reasonably offer?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Offer packing help and ask her aunt to confirm the ride directly with the other parent.", "consequence": "They divide the tasks: Nia packs the bag with her brother; her aunt contacts the parent and shares the arrangements. This keeps Nia involved without making her responsible for an unconfirmed ride. They set a deadline tonight to know whether the plan works."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Offer to send the first message, with her aunt handling confirmation and pickup details.", "consequence": "Nia drafts a clear request, and her aunt reviews and sends it. The parent agrees to the ride but asks about pickup. Nia points back to their division of tasks rather than accepting another job automatically. Asking for help works best when everyone knows who owns the next step."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Promise she will arrange everything, even if nobody replies.", "consequence": "The silence after the message makes Nia anxious. She cannot guarantee another person's availability. She tells her aunt what she has tried and asks for a backup plan before bedtime. Her willingness to help is real, but it does not give her control over every part of the situation."}]}, {"title": "The comment at the door", "text": ["The ride is confirmed. As Nia finishes packing, her brother says, ‘You made a big deal because you don't want to help me.’ She feels the urge to list every favor she has ever done. That would turn tonight into a different argument."], "question": "How should Nia respond without giving up her boundary?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain that caring about him and keeping her interview can both be true.", "consequence": "‘I want you to get there. That's why we're packing. My interview matters too.’ He does not immediately apologize, but he helps find his uniform. Nia does not need him to agree with every feeling before she can keep a reasonable commitment."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Pause the conversation and return to it when they are calmer.", "consequence": "Nia says, ‘I'm getting frustrated. Let's finish the bag, and we can talk tomorrow.’ Taking a pause is not the same as abandoning him. She follows through the next day, explaining why being counted in family planning matters to her."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell him he ruins all her plans.", "consequence": "He drops the uniform and walks away. Nia recognizes that her frustration became an attack. She repairs the words: ‘That wasn't fair. I'm upset about the scheduling, not about you.’ She can apologize for the insult without canceling her interview."}]}, {"title": "A place on the calendar", "text": ["Before bed, Nia and her aunt add both events to the calendar and agree to check it before assigning rides. Saturday is still busy. The interview is not a guaranteed job, and the family will face other conflicts. But Nia's time is part of the plan now, not a blank space everyone can fill.", "Think back: Which words protected Nia's opportunity while recognizing her family's needs? What would she do if the ride fell through? Self-advocacy can include helping, asking for support, and saying exactly what you cannot promise."]}]}, {"id": "school-missing-credit", "title": "Whose Name Is on It?", "category": "School drama", "teaser": "A group presentation is almost ready. The slide history tells a different story from the group chat.", "skill": "Seeking fair treatment with evidence and a clear request", "scenes": [{"title": "One slide before the bell", "text": ["Eli opens the shared presentation before class and stops at the title slide. Only two names are listed. His is missing. He spent Sunday checking sources and building the comparison chart, then sent his work to the group. Now the chart is there, cleaned up and recolored, with no mention of his contribution.", "Tessa has done most of the design. Marcus plans to speak. In the group chat, Marcus wrote, ‘We basically did this ourselves.’ Eli missed one after-school meeting because his ride could not wait. He told them ahead of time, but he wonders whether they think that means he did nothing.", "The teacher is helping another student. Presentations begin tomorrow. Eli wants credit without turning the classroom into an audience for a fight."]}, {"title": "The message before the message", "text": ["Eli starts typing, ‘You two are fake.’ Then he stops. That would express his anger, but it would not show what he contributed or explain what he wants changed. He has the original chart, the message he sent, and the shared file's history.", "Tessa looks over. ‘What? We thought you weren't presenting.’ Eli realizes they may be arguing about two different things: doing the work and speaking in front of the class."], "question": "What should Eli do first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Show his contribution and ask why his name was removed.", "consequence": "Eli points to the chart and his dated message. Tessa says she thought the names listed the speakers, not all contributors. Marcus disagrees about how much Eli did. The explanation is not settled, but naming specific work gives them something concrete to discuss instead of trading accusations."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the teacher privately to help clarify contribution and presentation expectations.", "consequence": "Eli explains the missing name and shows his work without claiming to know the others' motives. The teacher offers a short group check-in. Seeking support is reasonable; Eli does not have to argue alone before he is allowed to ask for help."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Post screenshots in the class chat so everyone can judge.", "consequence": "Several classmates respond before anyone checks the context. Tessa becomes defensive, and the disagreement spreads. Eli stops posting and asks the teacher for a private conversation. Evidence can support a fair request, but broadcasting a conflict can make resolving it harder."}]}, {"title": "Credit is not the only question", "text": ["During a short check-in, the teacher confirms that everyone should receive credit for documented work. Each student must also explain part of the project. Eli's name returns to the slide. Then Marcus hands him a full page: ‘Fine. Read this tomorrow.’", "Eli knows his research, but reading a long unfamiliar script aloud is difficult for him. He does not want to explain personal learning information to the whole group. He does need a workable way to show what he knows."], "question": "What request could help Eli participate?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask to explain his chart using brief notes and practice the handoff with the group.", "consequence": "The teacher says the goal is explaining the research, not reading a script word for word. Eli prepares three notes about the chart and asks where his section starts. A specific alternative helps the group plan while giving him a meaningful role."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Speak privately with the teacher about a workable presentation option.", "consequence": "Eli asks what options meet the assignment's goal and explains what makes the script difficult. They agree on notes and a brief rehearsal. He can request support without sharing private details with classmates. The teacher still expects him to demonstrate his understanding."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Accept the script without mentioning that he cannot prepare it in time.", "consequence": "At rehearsal Eli loses his place, and the others become impatient. He asks to pause and explains that the format is the obstacle, not a lack of knowledge. They revise his part around the chart. Speaking earlier would have saved time, but he can still request a useful change."}]}, {"title": "One more comment", "text": ["After rehearsal, Marcus mutters, ‘Must be nice getting your own rules.’ Eli has a clearer plan now, but the comment makes him feel singled out. He wants the project finished, not another round of proving that he belongs."], "question": "How can Eli protect his participation?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "State the agreed plan and ask Marcus to stop the comments.", "consequence": "Eli says, ‘The teacher approved this way to explain my work. Let's stick to our sections without comments about me.’ Marcus rolls his eyes. Eli keeps the boundary brief and does not argue for approval. If the remarks continue, he will report the specific comments privately."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Move away and tell the teacher what was said, asking for a respectful rehearsal.", "consequence": "The teacher schedules a supervised run-through and addresses respectful collaboration. Eli does not have to confront Marcus directly to advocate for himself. Asking for a concrete condition, rather than demanding a punishment, keeps the focus on what he needs to participate."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Refuse to share his chart unless Marcus apologizes immediately.", "consequence": "The project stalls, and Tessa is caught between them. Eli realizes he has made participation depend on a reaction he cannot control. He restores access to the chart and asks the teacher to help address the comment separately. Fair credit and respectful treatment still matter."}]}, {"title": "More than a name", "text": ["The next day, Eli explains the chart with his notes. He pauses once, checks his point, and continues. The presentation is not perfect, but it shows what he knows. The teacher reviews each person's contribution instead of relying only on the loudest account of who did the work.", "Eli and Marcus are not suddenly friends. What changed was the process: evidence, a specific request, and support when the conversation was not working. Which choice helped Eli keep both credit and participation? What could the group agree on before its next project?"]}]}, {"id": "pressure-phone", "title": "Everybody Was Looking", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "A phone reminder becomes a public standoff. Can Leon protect his dignity without making the problem bigger?", "skill": "Requesting privacy, managing escalation, and repairing a conversation", "scenes": [{"title": "One message, twenty witnesses", "text": ["Leon feels his phone buzz as the class starts independent work. His older sister is supposed to pick him up today, but her shift changed. He checks the screen to see whether he has a ride. Before he finishes reading, Mr. Ellis calls across the room: ‘Leon, phone away. We've already discussed this.’", "A few students look up. Someone laughs. Leon hears the words, but what lands hardest is the feeling that everybody has been invited into his business. He wants to explain the message. He also wants the teacher to stop speaking to him as if he is the only person who ever breaks a rule.", "‘You don't even know what I'm doing,’ Leon says. Mr. Ellis steps closer. ‘Then tell me without the attitude.’ Now both of them are frustrated, and the class is watching for the next line."]}, {"title": "An audience changes the conversation", "text": ["Leon can feel his jaw tighten. He could deliver a comeback that gets a laugh, but a laugh would not arrange his ride. He needs a way to explain the urgent message and address being called out without turning the room into a contest.", "He does not have to pretend the public correction felt fine. He does need to choose a next step that gives the conversation somewhere useful to go."], "question": "What should Leon try first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Put the phone down and ask to explain the transportation message privately.", "consequence": "Leon says, ‘It's about my ride. Can I explain quietly instead of across the room?’ Mr. Ellis lowers his voice. The phone issue is not automatically excused, but Leon has identified the need and requested a less public conversation. Putting the phone down buys room to talk; it does not mean his concern disappeared."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for a brief pause because he is too frustrated to explain clearly.", "consequence": "‘I need a minute so I don't say this wrong,’ Leon says. Mr. Ellis asks him to sit at the side table while the class starts. Leon uses the pause to identify two points: the ride message and wanting corrections handled privately. A pause is useful because he intends to return to the issue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Call out the teacher for embarrassing him in front of everyone.", "consequence": "Leon raises his voice: ‘You always try to embarrass me.’ Mr. Ellis starts defending himself, and students stop working. Leon's concern is real, but the public accusation makes it harder to hear. He stops the back-and-forth and requests a private check-in rather than trying to win the room."}]}, {"title": "Two problems, not one", "text": ["At the side of the room, Leon explains the pickup change. Mr. Ellis says the phone rule is intended to keep students working, not prevent transportation arrangements. He offers a brief check with the office if the ride is uncertain.", "Leon also wants to discuss how the correction happened. He worries that bringing it up will sound like an excuse. He can acknowledge his own words and still make a request about how they speak to each other."], "question": "How can Leon address both issues?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain the practical need, then ask for a quiet reminder next time.", "consequence": "‘I need to confirm my ride. And when I'm called across the room, I get embarrassed. Could you give me a quiet reminder?’ Mr. Ellis agrees to try that when possible and asks Leon to signal urgent messages before checking them. They have a specific plan instead of a promise that nobody will ever feel annoyed."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask a counselor to help them agree on a plan after class.", "consequence": "Leon says he wants help explaining the pattern without another argument. Mr. Ellis agrees to a short meeting. Support is not reserved for emergencies or for students who have already handled everything perfectly. Leon writes down what happened and what he wants changed so the meeting can focus on specifics."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say he will follow the rule only if the teacher apologizes first.", "consequence": "Mr. Ellis says they can discuss the correction, but class expectations still apply. The conversation stalls around who must move first. Leon changes the request: ‘I want us to talk about how that happened. Can we also settle the ride issue now?’ He can seek accountability without making every practical step depend on an immediate apology."}]}, {"title": "The comment on the way back", "text": ["Leon confirms his ride through the agreed process. As he returns to his seat, a classmate whispers, ‘You let him punk you.’ Leon feels the pressure to prove he has not lost. But the classmate will not deal with the consequences of Leon's next move."], "question": "What response serves Leon's goal now?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say the problem is handled and return to his work.", "consequence": "‘I got my ride sorted. I'm good,’ Leon says. The classmate has no dramatic reply to build on. Leon still feels irritated, but he does not need to perform an argument to prove he has dignity. He starts the first problem and saves any remaining concern for the planned conversation."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Move seats or ask for space rather than engage with the comment.", "consequence": "Leon asks to work at an open table. Changing the immediate situation helps him avoid another confrontation. He can explain the classmate's comment privately if it continues. Getting space is a choice about what helps him participate, not a statement that the comment was acceptable."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Restart the argument to show he is not afraid.", "consequence": "Leon calls toward Mr. Ellis, and the lesson stops again. He notices he is now reacting to the classmate instead of solving his original problem. He asks to reset: ‘I shouldn't have restarted that. I still want our check-in later.’ Repair can be direct without becoming a speech about being a bad person."}]}, {"title": "A quieter agreement", "text": ["Later, Leon and Mr. Ellis review a simple plan: signal urgent transportation messages, use the agreed process to check them, and handle concerns privately whenever possible. Neither promises never to get frustrated. They agree on what to do when frustration shows up.", "Leon protected more than a phone message. He practiced naming a need, asking for privacy, and returning to the issue without an audience. Which choice made his real concern easier to hear? What could he do if the agreed plan is repeatedly ignored?"]}], "artCategory": "School drama"}, {"id": "pressure-pointless", "title": "Why Am I Even Here?", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "A transition session interrupts an important assignment. Micah wants his time respected, but his first words shut down the conversation.", "skill": "Explaining priorities, requesting relevant support, and setting respectful boundaries", "scenes": [{"title": "Pulled at the wrong moment", "text": ["Micah has finally understood the science assignment when the office sends for him. He is supposed to meet Ms. Grant for a transition session. The worksheet on his desk is due before the end of the day, and he has already missed part of the teacher's explanation once this week.", "In the meeting room, Ms. Grant places a career activity on the table. ‘Let's start with what you're interested in after graduation.’ Micah pushes the paper away. ‘This is pointless. I'd rather be doing actual work.’ He expects another adult to tell him that his priorities do not matter.", "Ms. Grant asks what work he is missing. Micah hears another question when he wants a solution. ‘I already told you. I'm not repeating myself.’ The session has barely begun, and neither of them is getting what they came for."]}, {"title": "The reason underneath the reaction", "text": ["Micah does not dislike every conversation about his future. He wants a job working with engines and has been asking about a training program. What he dislikes is being surprised by a meeting that seems disconnected from today's responsibilities.", "Ms. Grant says, ‘We can talk about the timing. I need to understand what you need.’ Micah can keep arguing that the session is pointless, or he can name the problem precisely enough to make a request."], "question": "What should Micah explain first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Name the assignment and deadline, then ask for a plan that protects his classwork.", "consequence": "‘Science is due today, and I need the explanation I was getting. Can we keep this short or arrange another time?’ Ms. Grant now has information she can act on. She cannot promise an immediate schedule change, but she can help him contact the teacher and understand what flexibility exists."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what the session is for and how it connects to his training goal.", "consequence": "Ms. Grant explains that the activity practices identifying support and asking useful questions at work or training. Micah says he wants examples about automotive programs rather than a generic career worksheet. Understanding the purpose does not erase the missed work, but it gives him a way to shape a useful session."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Refuse every question until someone sends him back.", "consequence": "The minutes pass without a plan, which is exactly what Micah feared. Ms. Grant offers a pause and asks him to write one thing he needs. He writes, ‘Time to finish science.’ Even when talking feels difficult, a clear written request can restart a stalled conversation."}]}, {"title": "A choice inside the limits", "text": ["Ms. Grant checks what can be adjusted. She cannot cancel all future sessions or promise that every meeting will happen at Micah's preferred time. Today, she can keep the activity brief and help him communicate with his teacher. They can also review future timing together.", "She offers two relevant tasks: draft a question for an automotive training program or practice requesting clarification on an unfamiliar work task. Micah still wants to leave, but these are closer to something he cares about."], "question": "What can Micah ask for next?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Choose the training question and agree on a short session with a clear ending.", "consequence": "They agree to spend ten minutes drafting two questions about entry requirements and hands-on practice, then review the science plan. A time boundary helps Micah know what he is committing to. He is participating in a defined task, not agreeing that any length of interruption is fine."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Request a planning meeting to review timing and select useful future activities.", "consequence": "Micah asks for his preferences and classroom deadlines to be included in planning. Ms. Grant records the request and explains who needs to help approve changes. Having a voice in the plan does not guarantee every preference, but it creates a process beyond arguing at each session."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Agree to anything just to make the discussion stop.", "consequence": "Micah starts the worksheet without explaining what he needs. Halfway through, he is angry again because the clock is moving. He speaks up: ‘I agreed too fast. Can we define the finish point?’ Correcting an unclear agreement is more effective than expecting Ms. Grant to guess his limit."}]}, {"title": "Firm is different from personal", "text": ["When Ms. Grant asks him to read his first question aloud, Micah says, ‘Can you even hear? I said I wanted to go back.’ She responds, ‘I can hear that you're frustrated. Personal comments aren't helping us solve this.’ Micah feels challenged, but his original concern still matters."], "question": "How can Micah reset without abandoning his concern?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Own the personal comment and restate his request about time.", "consequence": "‘That comment wasn't fair. I'm worried about finishing science. How many minutes are left?’ Ms. Grant checks the agreed finish time. Micah's repair addresses his words, not his right to be concerned. They return to a question they can actually answer."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for a short quiet pause, then put his request in writing.", "consequence": "Micah takes a minute and writes, ‘I need a clear ending and help catching up.’ Ms. Grant responds to those two requests. A different communication method can help when a conversation is getting heated. He still needs to return to the discussion, and he does."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Keep making personal comments because being polite did not fix the timing.", "consequence": "Ms. Grant pauses the activity and seeks support to reset the meeting. More time passes without progress on science. Micah recognizes that intensity is not the same as effectiveness. He can still make a firm request and report concerns about scheduling without attacking the person in front of him."}]}, {"title": "Something worth coming back for", "text": ["Micah leaves with two training-program questions and a clear plan to address his missed classwork with the teacher. Ms. Grant notes his request for earlier notice and a scheduling review. The next meeting still needs coordination, and Micah is not suddenly excited about every activity.", "What changed was his influence on the conversation. ‘This is pointless’ became ‘I need relevant work and a plan for what I'm missing.’ Which response helped adults understand the actual problem? If the plan does not happen, who could Micah ask to help review it?"]}], "artCategory": "School drama"}, {"id": "pressure-last-word", "title": "The Last Word", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "A notification turns a normal afternoon into a public challenge. Winning the argument could cost more than walking away.", "skill": "Recognizing escalation · Setting boundaries · Seeking support", "scenes": [{"title": "Everybody is watching", "text": ["By the time Micah leaves practice, the clip has already reached three group chats. It shows him missing an easy shot. Somebody has slowed it down, added laughing sounds, and tagged him. He could live with the missed shot. The caption underneath is what makes his hands tighten around his phone.", "“All that talking and this is you?” wrote Trent. Yesterday, Micah asked Trent to stop interrupting during their group presentation. Now the basketball clip feels like a reply delivered in front of everyone. Two friends message him: “You gonna let him do that?”", "Micah starts typing something about Trent’s family. He knows exactly which sentence would hurt. Then a younger teammate looks over his shoulder. The message is still unsent. Micah wants the disrespect to stop, not to spend tomorrow explaining why he made it worse."]}, {"title": "The message you can keep", "text": ["Micah deletes the family comment, but the anger stays. His friends keep sending screenshots. He does not owe the group a performance, even if refusing one feels embarrassing. He needs to choose what happens to his attention next.", "His bus arrives in ten minutes. That gives him a short window to lower the temperature before another notification decides for him."], "question": "What does Micah do first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Send one boundary: “Take the clip and tag down. I’m not arguing in here.” Then mute the chat.", "consequence": "Trent replies with another joke, but Micah does not answer. The boundary is clear and the mute stops the constant interruptions. Muting will not remove the post, so Micah saves the link privately in case he needs help reporting it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Save the post, report the targeted harassment, and leave without making an announcement.", "consequence": "Leaving gives Micah breathing room, although one friend calls it backing down. The report may take time. He decides whose messages he actually wants to receive tonight instead of letting the whole group follow him home."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Put the phone away and ask a trusted teammate to walk with him to the bus.", "consequence": "His teammate listens without demanding a comeback. Micah gets through the hottest part of the moment without sending anything. The post still needs addressing later, but pausing has protected his ability to choose instead of react."}]}, {"title": "A hallway audience", "text": ["The next morning, Trent calls across the hall, “No reply?” A few students stop walking. Micah feels his face heat up. Trent is several steps away, and a teacher is near the classroom door.", "Micah has a class to reach. He can refuse the confrontation without pretending he likes what happened."], "question": "How does he handle the public challenge?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say, “I’m going to class. Stop tagging me,” and keep walking toward the teacher.", "consequence": "Someone laughs, which stings. Micah still reaches class without turning the hallway into a contest. He tells the teacher what happened privately so there is context if Trent follows or continues."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Skip the reply and approach the teacher: “He’s trying to start something. Can I come in now?”", "consequence": "The teacher lets him inside and checks what support he needs. Micah worries that people will talk, but he has chosen access to help over an audience. He can explain the online part once he is calmer."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell his friend, “Walk with me. I’m not doing this,” and leave by the open route.", "consequence": "His friend follows instead of joining the crowd. They do not circle back for a final comment. Micah plans a private check-in with staff because getting away once does not automatically stop repeated targeting."}]}, {"title": "The line changes", "text": ["At lunch, a new message names a place after school and tells Micah to show up. It could be bluffing, but Micah cannot safely assume that. A screenshot is useful; going there to find out is not.", "He needs an adult involved now, without forwarding the invitation around for entertainment."], "question": "Which safety plan does Micah choose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Show school staff the message and ask for help arranging a safe departure.", "consequence": "Staff assess the message and arrange support. Micah explains what was said, without adding guesses about motives. He may not control every next step, but he asks who will be told and how he can avoid contact."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Call his caregiver from the office and show staff the message while they make a pickup plan.", "consequence": "His caregiver helps with transport while staff handle the school safety concern. Micah stays in a supervised place. Sharing the exact message gives the adults more to work with than saying there is just “drama.”"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask a trusted teacher to accompany him to the counselor or administrator right away.", "consequence": "He does not have to explain the whole story alone. The teacher helps him reach the people who can assess risk and plan departure. Micah does not reply to the challenge or agree to meet."}]}, {"title": "Not another round", "text": ["That evening, Micah still wants an apology. Getting support has not made the disrespect acceptable or erased his anger. It has kept an online argument from choosing where he goes and what he does.", "He texts the friend who walked with him: “Thanks for not hyping it up.” Then he puts his phone down. Tomorrow’s plan includes checking in with staff, not checking whether Trent has found a new audience.", "Reflect: Which action protected Micah without requiring him to pretend he was fine? Write a firm boundary that does not include an insult. What changed when the message proposed an in-person confrontation?"]}], "artCategory": "Friendships"}, {"id": "pressure-on-the-clock", "title": "On the Clock", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "A supervisor corrects the wrong person in front of customers. The next sentence could change the whole shift.", "skill": "Responding to feedback · Asking for evidence · Escalating concerns professionally", "scenes": [{"title": "Not my mistake", "text": ["Samara has been working at a neighborhood market for three weeks. She knows the closing checklist and keeps a photo of it on her phone. Today, the chilled drinks display has empty spaces, the checkout line is growing, and somebody has left a cart in the aisle.", "Her supervisor, Ms. Alvarez, points at the display. “We already talked about keeping this stocked. You need to pay attention.” Two customers look over. Samara had been assigned to checkout for the last hour. Another employee was handling drinks.", "Samara feels the reply forming: maybe the supervisor should pay attention to her own assignments. She needs this job, but she also needs not to become the person blamed whenever something goes wrong. Keeping the job should not mean agreeing to something she did not do."]}, {"title": "One sentence first", "text": ["The customers are waiting. Ms. Alvarez looks ready to move on. Samara has seconds to choose a response that makes space for facts without starting a public argument.", "She can address the immediate task and still question the correction. Those are not the same conversation."], "question": "What does Samara say?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I’ve been on checkout. Should I stay here or restock first? Can we discuss the assignment privately?”", "consequence": "The supervisor tells her to stay at checkout and agrees to talk later. Samara has not accepted blame; she has clarified priorities. She notes the time so she can return to the conversation instead of letting it disappear."}, {"label": "B", "text": "“I want to understand. Are you correcting today’s assignment or something from another shift?”", "consequence": "Ms. Alvarez pauses and says she thought Samara had drinks today. That may explain the mistake, but it does not erase the embarrassment. Samara asks to finish clarifying once the line is shorter."}, {"label": "C", "text": "“I can help. I need a minute to settle down before we discuss what happened.”", "consequence": "The supervisor assigns another employee to the display and arranges a brief reset when coverage arrives. Samara stays at her station until covered. Asking for a pause avoids abandoning work while protecting the next conversation."}]}, {"title": "The private conversation", "text": ["After the rush, Ms. Alvarez says she may have mixed up assignments. Then she adds, “But you looked annoyed.” Samara was annoyed. She does not want the discussion to become only about her face while the original accusation disappears.", "She opens the checklist and thinks about what she needs: correct expectations and feedback she can actually use."], "question": "How does she respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I was frustrated because I was corrected for another assignment. Here’s what I was given. What should I do differently next time?”", "consequence": "They compare the checklist and confirm the assignment mix-up. Ms. Alvarez also explains that Samara should call for backup sooner when the line grows. Samara can use that specific feedback without accepting responsibility for the display."}, {"label": "B", "text": "“Please give me corrections privately when possible. I can listen better without customers watching.”", "consequence": "Ms. Alvarez agrees to handle nonurgent feedback privately, but explains that urgent directions still happen on the floor. They separate immediate instructions from personal criticism and agree on a quick phrase for requesting a later conversation."}, {"label": "C", "text": "“Can we write down who owns each closing task? I want us checking the same information.”", "consequence": "They add names beside the tasks. A shared checklist cannot prevent every misunderstanding, but it reduces guessing. Samara still says the public correction bothered her rather than hoping the new form communicates that for her."}]}, {"title": "It happens again", "text": ["A week later, another supervisor gives Samara a task that conflicts with the posted list. She asks which one comes first and gets, “Just do your job.” Her first impulse is to walk out.", "Samara knows one difficult exchange and a repeated pattern need different responses. She wants clear instructions, not a contest over who sounds tougher."], "question": "What is her next step?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask the shift lead to resolve the conflicting priorities, using the list and the exact instructions.", "consequence": "The lead chooses the urgent task and covers the other. Samara follows the clarified plan. She writes a factual note afterward, separating what was said from what she thinks the supervisor meant."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Request a private meeting with the manager about repeated unclear instructions and public corrections.", "consequence": "She brings dates and examples, plus a request for consistent assignments. The manager may need time to investigate. Samara asks what to do during the next conflict and when they will check back, rather than expecting instant agreement."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask a workplace mentor to help her prepare a concise concern and identify the appropriate reporting route.", "consequence": "The mentor helps her organize facts without exaggeration. Samara raises the concern through the workplace process and keeps doing tasks she understands safely. Getting help with wording does not mean surrendering her own account."}]}, {"title": "More than staying quiet", "text": ["Samara finishes the shift with a clearer list and a meeting time. She has not promised to tolerate every correction forever. She has built a record, asked for specific changes, and kept options open.", "On the way home, she practices one sentence: “I want to do this correctly; which instruction takes priority?” It sounds calmer than what she first wanted to say, but it is not weaker.", "Reflect: Which feedback was useful, and which concern still needed addressing? Write a question that clarifies an unfair-seeming correction. Explain when a private conversation might need to become a documented concern."]}], "artCategory": "Work life"}, {"id": "pressure-doorway", "title": "The Doorway Argument", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "One missed text, an unfinished chore, and a tired household turn a small disagreement into a standoff.", "skill": "Naming frustration · Negotiating limits · Following through", "scenes": [{"title": "Before the door closes", "text": ["Andre reaches home twenty minutes late. His bus connection was delayed, his phone battery died, and the first thing he hears is his uncle asking why the trash is still in the kitchen. Andre had promised to take it out before leaving. He forgot.", "His uncle has just finished a long shift. He says Andre cannot go to Saturday’s game if this is how he handles responsibility. Andre drops his backpack harder than he meant to. “You didn’t even ask what happened.” His uncle hears the tone before the words.", "Andre has a real explanation for being late and a real unfinished chore. Neither cancels the other. He wants his uncle to recognize the difference, but the doorway is becoming a place where both of them are trying to win the last sentence."]}, {"title": "Room to talk", "text": ["Andre notices his voice getting louder. He is tired of feeling like every mistake proves something about his character. His uncle says, “Don’t walk away while I’m talking.”", "He needs a way to lower the pressure without disappearing or agreeing that everything is his fault."], "question": "What does Andre try?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I forgot the trash. I’ll do it now. Then can I explain why I was late?”", "consequence": "The chore gets finished, which removes one immediate issue. His uncle is still upset about the missed contact. Andre returns as promised and asks to discuss that separately instead of treating the completed chore as a pass for everything."}, {"label": "B", "text": "“I’m getting loud, and I don’t want to make this worse. Can we talk in ten minutes in the kitchen?”", "consequence": "His uncle agrees to a short pause. Andre uses it to drink water and organize the facts, not send angry messages. Returning at the agreed time shows that the pause was preparation, not an escape from responsibility."}, {"label": "C", "text": "“I hear you’re worried. The bus was late and my phone died. I also forgot the chore. Those are two different things.”", "consequence": "Naming both parts helps his uncle hear the explanation, although the tone is still tense. Andre keeps the account brief. He offers to show the bus information later rather than demanding immediate belief in the doorway."}]}, {"title": "What the rule is for", "text": ["At the table, his uncle explains that no message meant no way to know where Andre was. Andre says the consequence felt decided before anyone listened. They disagree about Saturday, but now they understand more than each other’s volume.", "Andre wants a plan for late buses that does not require a phone to work perfectly every time."], "question": "What agreement does he propose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Build a backup-contact plan and ask to reconsider Saturday after he follows through on his responsibilities.", "consequence": "They identify a safe place to request a call and an agreed contact number. His uncle does not immediately reverse the consequence. Andre asks when they can revisit it, giving the plan a clear check-in rather than an endless argument."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for a predictable rule: what happens for a forgotten chore, and what happens for an unavoidable transport delay?", "consequence": "The conversation separates responsibility from circumstances. They agree that informing someone matters even when the delay is not his fault. Andre gets clearer expectations, though both acknowledge that emergencies may require flexibility."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Explain what felt unfair, then propose a smaller consequence connected to the unfinished responsibility.", "consequence": "His uncle considers an extra household task instead of missing the game. Negotiation is not guaranteed, but Andre gives a concrete alternative. He can disagree with a decision without insulting the person making it."}]}, {"title": "Friday changes the plan", "text": ["On Friday, a friend offers Andre a ride after an event. The pickup time could get him home later than the new agreement. His friend says, “Just text when we leave.” Andre remembers how much harder it was to negotiate after the problem.", "He wants to go. He also wants the new agreement to mean something when it becomes inconvenient."], "question": "How does he handle the change?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask in advance for a specific later time, sharing the ride plan and a backup.", "consequence": "His uncle asks questions and approves a limited extension. Andre confirms the driver, timing, and backup before leaving. Permission for this evening is not a permanent change, so he does not assume next weekend will be the same."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Keep the original return time and arrange an earlier, dependable ride.", "consequence": "He misses the final part of the event and feels disappointed. He tells his friend without blaming his uncle. Choosing predictability tonight does not prevent asking to change the rule during a calmer conversation later."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Explain the invitation and ask to compare options together before committing to his friend.", "consequence": "They find that the proposed ride is uncertain and choose a different pickup. Andre has brought a real problem instead of a finished decision. If no workable option existed, he would still need to choose whether to attend within the agreed limits."}]}, {"title": "A quieter entrance", "text": ["When Andre gets home, he puts his backpack down and checks the chore list. The household is not suddenly free of arguments. But the next disagreement has a better starting place than guesses about what the other person meant.", "He can own a mistake without accepting a label. He can express frustration without making the loudest sentence his only message. The agreement works because both people know what was promised and when to revisit it.", "Reflect: Which part was Andre responsible for, and which part was outside his control? Write a request that includes a reason and a specific plan. You can discuss this fictional household without sharing private experiences."]}], "artCategory": "Home life"}, {"id": "relationship-unsent", "title": "The Message You Didn't Send", "category": "Relationships", "teaser": "A canceled call becomes three different stories in two people's heads.", "skill": "Communicating feelings and checking assumptions", "scenes": [{"title": "Seen at 8:42", "text": ["Nico and Avery usually call after dinner. Tonight Nico sends a picture of the stage set he helped build and asks whether Avery wants to hear about rehearsal. The message says Seen. Twenty minutes pass. Then a photo appears in a friend's story: Avery at a table with three classmates, laughing.", "Nico decides Avery is avoiding him. Avery, meanwhile, thinks Nico knows about the study group mentioned yesterday. The restaurant is noisy, and Avery plans to call afterward. Neither person knows what the other has assumed.", "When Avery finally texts, ‘How was rehearsal?’ Nico replies, ‘Don't worry about it.’ He wants Avery to notice that something hurt. Instead, Avery reads the short answer as a request for space."]}, {"title": "A test nobody agreed to take", "text": ["The next morning, Avery asks why Nico seems distant. Nico is tempted to say, ‘If you cared, you'd know.’ But the thing he wants understood is more specific: he expected a call and felt brushed aside when the message went unanswered."], "question": "How can Nico make the real issue clear?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Describe what happened and ask whether they had different expectations.", "consequence": "‘I thought we'd call. When I saw the photo, I felt left out. Did we have different plans?’ Avery explains the study group. The explanation does not erase Nico's disappointment, but they now have facts to discuss instead of competing guesses about who cares more."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Write a short message about feeling disappointed, then ask to talk after class.", "consequence": "Nico finds writing easier than speaking in the hallway. He names the missed call without accusing Avery of deliberately hurting him. They agree on a time to talk. Asking for a calmer setting gives both people a better chance to understand the message."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stay cold and see whether Avery can figure it out.", "consequence": "Avery gives Nico more space, believing that is what he wants. Nico becomes more upset. He realizes silence has not communicated the reason. He can still say, ‘I wasn't clear earlier. I wanted to talk about last night,’ rather than making the misunderstanding another test."}]}, {"title": "An explanation is not a reset button", "text": ["Avery says, ‘I wasn't ignoring you. I can't always answer right away.’ Nico agrees, but he still wants predictable plans. They need an agreement that respects both connection and time apart, not a rule that someone must always be available."], "question": "What agreement could work for both of them?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Confirm planned calls and send a brief update when a definite plan changes.", "consequence": "They distinguish a scheduled call from an ordinary message. If they have agreed on a time, they will try to communicate changes. Neither promises instant replies all day. The agreement is specific enough to use without turning their phones into attendance trackers."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what communication each person finds comfortable and look for overlap.", "consequence": "Avery prefers short texts on busy evenings. Nico likes a longer conversation a few times a week. They choose two planned calls and leave other messages flexible. Different preferences do not automatically mean different levels of care, but they need honest discussion."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask for read receipts and constant updates so uncertainty never happens again.", "consequence": "Avery says that would feel like monitoring. Nico notices that more information cannot guarantee he will never feel insecure. He revises his request toward confirming actual plans. He can ask for reliability without requiring access to every moment of Avery's day."}]}, {"title": "The next delay", "text": ["A few days later, Avery is late for a planned call. Nico feels the old story forming: Nothing changed. Before he sends an angry message, he remembers they agreed to talk about specific events rather than treat one delay as proof of everything."], "question": "What should Nico do now?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Send one check-in and say how long he is available tonight.", "consequence": "Nico writes, ‘Are we still calling? I'm free until nine.’ Avery replies that a family errand ran late and apologizes. They reschedule. Nico has communicated his availability without waiting indefinitely or turning the delay into an accusation."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Continue his evening and discuss reliability when they next talk.", "consequence": "Nico starts his homework instead of repeatedly checking the screen. Later he explains that missed plans matter to him. Taking care of his own time does not prevent him from addressing a pattern if one develops."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Send several messages accusing Avery of not caring.", "consequence": "Avery returns to a string of accusations and becomes defensive. Nico owns the accusation: ‘I assumed your reason instead of asking.’ He can repair that while still discussing the missed call. An apology for his words does not cancel his need for dependable plans."}]}, {"title": "Say the part that matters", "text": ["They are still learning each other's habits. No agreement makes them mind readers or prevents every disappointment. What helps is naming the event, the feeling, and the request without treating a guess as a fact.", "Which choice gave Nico useful information? What would he do if the agreement kept being ignored? Communication can clarify a misunderstanding, and it can also reveal whether a relationship meets both people's needs."]}], "artCategory": "Relationships"}, {"id": "relationship-password", "title": "If You Trust Me", "category": "Relationships", "teaser": "A request for a password turns into a question about privacy and proof.", "skill": "Protecting digital privacy and recognizing pressure", "scenes": [{"title": "A phone between them", "text": ["Sasha and Drew are waiting for the activity bus when Sasha's phone lights up. Drew sees a message preview from an unfamiliar name. It is someone from Sasha's art group asking about a project, but Drew's expression changes.", "‘We should exchange passwords,’ Drew says. ‘Couples shouldn't have secrets.’ Sasha does not want to share an account password. The account includes family conversations, school messages, and friends' private stories. Sasha also worries that refusing will sound like an admission of wrongdoing.", "Drew holds out a phone: ‘I'll give you mine. What's the problem?’ Sasha likes Drew. That does not make the request comfortable."]}, {"title": "Privacy is not a confession", "text": ["Sasha could explain the project message, but the bigger issue is access to an entire account. A boundary does not become unreasonable because another person offers to cross the same boundary."], "question": "How can Sasha respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say no to sharing passwords and offer to discuss the actual worry.", "consequence": "‘I don't share passwords. I can talk about what's worrying you.’ Drew says the unknown name felt threatening. Sasha can hear that feeling without giving account access. A conversation about trust is different from a requirement to surrender privacy."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for time and discuss the request with a trusted adult before deciding.", "consequence": "Sasha says, ‘I'm not deciding under pressure.’ A counselor later helps identify the difference between voluntary sharing and being made to prove innocence. Taking time is allowed. Sasha does not owe an immediate answer simply because Drew is upset."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Share the password to stop the disagreement.", "consequence": "The tension briefly eases, but Sasha becomes anxious about other people's messages being read. Sharing once does not remove the right to change the boundary. Sasha can secure the account and seek help planning that step if Drew's reaction feels threatening. The pressure is not Sasha's fault."}]}, {"title": "The answer after no", "text": ["When Sasha restates the boundary, Drew says, ‘Then maybe you're hiding something. Everybody does this.’ Sasha feels pulled toward defending every message. That would leave the underlying demand untouched."], "question": "What should Sasha do with the repeated pressure?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Repeat the boundary once and end the conversation if the demand continues.", "consequence": "‘My answer is no. We can discuss trust, but I won't argue about giving you access.’ Sasha moves toward the bus group. A boundary includes what Sasha will do next. Drew's disappointment does not create a right to inspect the account."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask a trusted person for support and avoid being alone for the next conversation.", "consequence": "Sasha tells an adult what Drew actually said, rather than minimizing it as ordinary drama. They plan a supported conversation and a safe way home. Support is reasonable before a situation becomes an emergency, especially when a clear no is not being respected."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Offer screenshots of every conversation instead of the password.", "consequence": "Drew starts asking about each name and missing message. The substitute has become another form of monitoring. Sasha can stop the exchange and say the arrangement does not work. No amount of proof can guarantee another person will stop demanding more."}]}, {"title": "A boundary gets tested", "text": ["That evening Drew sends, ‘If you loved me, this wouldn't be a problem.’ Sasha's friend says not to make a big deal out of it. Sasha is allowed to take the repeated pressure seriously, even if someone else would make a different choice."], "question": "What next step protects Sasha's choice?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "State that affection does not require account access and pause contact for the evening.", "consequence": "Sasha sends one clear message and stops debating. The pause gives room to think about whether Drew can respect the boundary. Sasha does not need to win a debate before taking space. If messages become threatening, support and safety take priority over replying."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Save the concerning messages privately and ask a trusted adult for help deciding next steps.", "consequence": "The adult helps Sasha consider account security, contact boundaries, and whether to continue the relationship. Saving messages is for support, not public retaliation. If there is an immediate threat, Sasha should get urgent local help rather than handle it alone."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Trade passwords after all, hoping the argument will finally end.", "consequence": "Sasha feels relief and unease at the same time. The decision can be changed. A trusted person can help Sasha secure the account and plan safely if access has already been shared. Being pressured into a choice does not mean Sasha deserves whatever happens afterward."}]}, {"title": "Trust without a login", "text": ["Sasha decides not to share account access. Whether the relationship continues depends partly on Drew's willingness to respect that choice, not on Sasha producing enough proof. A healthy discussion can include insecurity. It cannot require someone to give up privacy to avoid punishment.", "Which responses protected a clear choice? Who could support Sasha if pressure increased? Private messages also belong to the people who sent them, not only the person holding the phone."]}], "artCategory": "Relationships"}, {"id": "relationship-plans", "title": "Two Things Can Matter", "category": "Relationships", "teaser": "An accepted summer opportunity collides with a couple's unspoken plans.", "skill": "Maintaining independence while negotiating shared plans", "scenes": [{"title": "The good news gets quiet", "text": ["Tori opens the acceptance message three times before showing it to anyone. She has a place in a two-week summer photography workshop. It is close enough to commute, but the days are full. She has wanted this since her art teacher first mentioned it.", "When she tells her boyfriend, Ben, he says, ‘So what about us?’ They had talked about spending more time together after finals. Tori thought that meant making plans around their other commitments. Ben thought it meant most afternoons together.", "Tori's excitement turns into guilt. She wants the workshop and the relationship. She does not want every independent plan to feel like a vote against Ben."]}, {"title": "A plan or an assumption?", "text": ["Ben reminds her that he turned down extra shifts because he expected to see her. Tori did not ask him to do that, but she understands why he feels disappointed. Neither of them can solve the disagreement by declaring the other person's feelings ridiculous."], "question": "What should Tori say first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Acknowledge his disappointment and explain that she intends to attend the workshop.", "consequence": "‘I see why you're disappointed. This workshop matters to me, and I'm going. Let's look at what we can plan.’ Tori states the decision without pretending it has no impact. Ben can have feelings about the change without controlling whether she takes the opportunity."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask which plans were actually confirmed and clarify what each person assumed.", "consequence": "They discover they agreed on one Saturday trip, not every weekday. Tori keeps the Saturday commitment and explains the workshop schedule. Checking specifics prevents an imagined agreement from becoming an obligation neither person knowingly accepted."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say she might quit the workshop just to end the tension.", "consequence": "Ben relaxes, but Tori feels resentful. Before withdrawing, she corrects herself: ‘I spoke because I felt pressured. I still want to attend.’ She can reconsider her words without being dishonest. A promise made to stop discomfort may not be a workable promise."}]}, {"title": "Room for both lives", "text": ["The workshop ends at four. Tori will need time to travel, eat, and finish assignments. Ben proposes meeting every evening. Tori knows that schedule would leave no rest or time with friends. They need realistic plans, not a replacement promise she cannot keep."], "question": "What arrangement could Tori offer?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Choose two evenings together and keep the remaining time flexible.", "consequence": "They put two specific plans on the calendar. Tori does not promise constant availability outside them. Ben knows when they will connect, and Tori has room for the workshop and other relationships. The agreement can be reviewed if it does not work for both people."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask Ben for his most important shared plan and build around that.", "consequence": "Ben says their Saturday trip matters more than daily visits. They protect that plan and discuss a short midweek call. Asking about priorities finds more room than assuming every request is equally important. Tori still keeps time for herself."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Promise every evening, assuming she can manage somehow.", "consequence": "By the third day, Tori is exhausted and cancels abruptly. Ben is frustrated by an agreement that was never realistic. Tori explains the problem and renegotiates. Being dependable sometimes means offering less at the beginning rather than repeatedly overpromising."}]}, {"title": "The photo from the workshop", "text": ["Tori posts a photo with her workshop group. Ben comments privately, ‘Looks like you don't miss me at all.’ Tori feels as though enjoying the opportunity has become something she must hide. She needs to address the comment, not apologize for having a good day."], "question": "How can Tori respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say that enjoying the workshop and caring about him can both be true.", "consequence": "Tori says, ‘I'm happy here, and I care about you. I don't want to feel guilty for enjoying it.’ Ben admits he feels left out. They can discuss connection without requiring Tori to look unhappy whenever they are apart."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Set a boundary around guilt-inducing comments and return to the agreed plans.", "consequence": "‘Please tell me you miss me directly instead of making my photo a problem.’ Tori reminds Ben of their next plan. If the comments become a pattern, she can seek support and reconsider whether the relationship leaves room for her independence."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stop sharing workshop photos so he will not get upset.", "consequence": "The immediate comment stops, but Tori starts shrinking a meaningful part of her life. She notices the cost and talks with a trusted person. Adjusting a social-media habit is her choice; feeling required to hide ordinary happiness to manage someone else's reaction deserves attention."}]}, {"title": "A wider picture", "text": ["Tori completes the workshop. She and Ben have some enjoyable plans and some difficult conversations. The opportunity was not a test of love. It was a chance to see whether they could respect two people's goals while sharing time together.", "Which choices kept Tori's plans realistic? How would she know compromise had become giving up too much? Independence is not the opposite of caring about someone."]}], "artCategory": "Relationships"}, {"id": "relationship-ending", "title": "A Clear Ending", "category": "Relationships", "teaser": "Rae wants to end a relationship kindly. Being kind does not mean negotiating away the decision.", "skill": "Communicating a breakup, respecting boundaries, and seeking support", "scenes": [{"title": "Knowing before saying", "text": ["Rae has been thinking for several weeks about ending the relationship with Morgan. Nothing dramatic happened in one moment. Rae no longer wants to date and has been avoiding plans rather than saying it clearly. Morgan has noticed and keeps asking what is wrong.", "A friend says Rae owes Morgan a face-to-face conversation. Another says to disappear and avoid the discomfort. Rae wants to be honest, but also remembers that Morgan has shouted during disagreements. Rae is not sure a private meeting alone would feel comfortable.", "Ending a relationship may hurt someone's feelings. Rae can care about that without needing permission to end it. The first task is choosing a clear, safe way to communicate."]}, {"title": "The setting matters", "text": ["Morgan asks to meet in an empty parking lot after practice. Rae feels uneasy. A breakup does not require a private setting, and there is no single communication method that is safest for every situation."], "question": "How should Rae arrange the conversation?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Choose a brief phone conversation with a trusted person nearby.", "consequence": "Rae tells a friend when the call will happen and prepares a clear sentence. Having support nearby does not make the decision less sincere. Rae can end the call if it becomes threatening or abusive rather than staying to prove the breakup was handled properly."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Choose a public place with an independent way home, if that feels safe.", "consequence": "Rae checks the plan with a trusted adult, chooses a populated setting, and does not depend on Morgan for transportation. The arrangement reduces isolation, though it cannot guarantee another person's behavior. If Rae feels unsafe, a message or supported alternative remains valid."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Agree to meet alone because otherwise Morgan might be offended.", "consequence": "Rae's unease grows. Rae changes the plan before going and tells a trusted person why. Avoiding someone's disappointment is not more important than safety. Choosing a safer method does not cause or excuse any angry response from Morgan."}]}, {"title": "Kind and unambiguous", "text": ["When the conversation starts, Morgan asks whether this is just a break. Rae is tempted to say maybe because a definite answer feels harsh. But Rae has already decided to end the relationship. False hope could make the next conversation harder."], "question": "What should Rae communicate?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "State the decision directly without listing personal attacks.", "consequence": "‘I don't want to continue dating. This is a breakup, not a break.’ Rae can acknowledge that it hurts without turning the decision into a debate about Morgan's worth. A clear statement gives accurate information, even when the other person does not welcome it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Use a prepared message to keep the decision clear when speaking feels difficult.", "consequence": "Rae reads a short note: the relationship is ending, the decision is final, and practical details can be handled later. Preparing words is a communication tool, not evidence that the feelings are fake. Rae avoids promising friendship that may not be wanted or possible."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say maybe they can try again soon, although Rae does not want that.", "consequence": "Morgan begins planning how to win Rae back. Rae realizes the reassurance created a different expectation. Rae corrects it: ‘I shouldn't have suggested that. I am ending the relationship.’ A difficult clarification is more respectful than continuing a promise Rae does not mean."}]}, {"title": "After the conversation", "text": ["Morgan sends repeated messages asking for one more meeting and says, ‘You owe me closure.’ Rae has already explained the decision. Some belongings still need to be returned, but that does not require reopening the relationship discussion."], "question": "What boundary can Rae set?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Send one practical message about returning belongings through a trusted person.", "consequence": "Rae specifies what will be returned and how, then stops discussing reconciliation. Practical contact can be limited to practical matters. Rae does not have to keep answering the same emotional demand because it is phrased as a need for closure."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask a trusted adult for help with repeated contact and adjust contact settings as needed.", "consequence": "Rae describes the messages and seeks a plan. If contact becomes threatening, stalking, or an immediate safety concern, urgent local support is appropriate. Rae can preserve concerning messages privately and choose not to respond. Morgan remains responsible for Morgan's behavior."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Keep meeting whenever Morgan asks to prevent hurt feelings.", "consequence": "Each meeting restarts the same negotiation and leaves Rae feeling trapped. Rae can stop agreeing and seek support for a firmer boundary. Caring that someone is upset does not make Rae responsible for removing every painful feeling or continuing an unwanted relationship."}]}, {"title": "An ending is allowed", "text": ["Rae returns the belongings through the agreed arrangement and takes space. There is sadness, relief, and uncertainty. None of those feelings mean the decision was invalid. Rae does not need to turn Morgan into a villain to know the relationship should end.", "Which choices made the message clear? What support would matter if the boundary were not respected? Kindness can shape the words, but safety and consent shape what happens next."]}], "artCategory": "Relationships"}, {"id": "relationship-friends", "title": "The Saturday Table", "category": "Relationships", "teaser": "Kai's weekly time with friends starts to feel like something he has to defend.", "skill": "Maintaining friendships and responding to jealousy without surrendering independence", "scenes": [{"title": "The invitation that isn't new", "text": ["Every other Saturday, Kai meets three friends at the community center to play games and work on music. They started going long before Kai began dating Jules. This week, Jules asks Kai to skip it and spend the afternoon together instead.", "Kai suggests Sunday. Jules says, ‘You always choose them.’ Kai checks the calendar in his head. They spent the last two Saturdays together because the center was closed. He feels accused of a pattern that does not match what happened.", "Jules has had a lonely week and wants reassurance. Kai can care about that without treating his friendships as evidence against the relationship. He wants both people to have lives beyond each other."]}, {"title": "What is the request?", "text": ["Kai could respond with a list of every hour they spent together. That might prove a point without addressing the feeling. He can also ask about the need without accepting the claim that seeing friends is a betrayal."], "question": "How should Kai begin?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Acknowledge the lonely week and keep his Saturday plan.", "consequence": "‘I hear that you want time together. I'm keeping Saturday with my friends, and I'm available Sunday.’ Kai offers connection without canceling a separate commitment. Jules may still be disappointed. A reasonable boundary does not require the other person to feel happy immediately."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what kind of connection Jules is missing, then discuss a specific plan.", "consequence": "Jules says their recent time together has been distracted. Kai proposes a phone-free Sunday walk. The discussion moves from competing for hours to identifying what feels meaningful. Kai does not have to reduce his friendships to improve the quality of their time together."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cancel because it seems easier than discussing jealousy.", "consequence": "The argument ends, but Kai feels resentful and his friends are disappointed. One changed plan is not automatically a problem; repeatedly canceling to manage someone else's jealousy can become one. Kai notices the pattern and can reopen the conversation about independent time."}]}, {"title": "An invitation is not supervision", "text": ["Jules asks to come to the center. Kai sometimes invites Jules, but this meeting includes a private conversation one friend requested. Jules says, ‘Why would it be private unless you're hiding something?’ Kai needs to respect his friend's privacy as well as his own choices."], "question": "How can Kai answer?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain that this gathering is friends-only and offer another time to meet the group.", "consequence": "Kai says, ‘This time isn't open, but we can plan something together another weekend.’ Friends may have private conversations without doing anything wrong. An invitation should be genuine, not a requirement to prove that Kai can be trusted."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Keep the boundary simple without sharing the friend's personal issue.", "consequence": "‘It's someone else's private conversation. I won't share their details.’ Kai protects information that is not his to disclose. He can discuss Jules's feelings separately, but explaining every secret belonging to a friend is not the price of keeping a relationship calm."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell Jules the friend's private issue to prove the gathering is innocent.", "consequence": "Jules understands the reason, but Kai has shared something without permission. Kai recognizes that proving innocence should not require exposing another person's confidence. He takes responsibility with his friend and changes how he responds to future demands for details."}]}, {"title": "The check-ins keep coming", "text": ["At the center, Kai receives several messages asking where he is, who is there, and why he has not replied. He already shared his general plan. The questions are making it hard to be present, and he feels watched rather than connected."], "question": "What should Kai do?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Send one clear availability message and put the phone away.", "consequence": "‘I'm with my friends until four. I'll talk later, but I won't keep checking in.’ Kai follows through. He is not required to provide a continuous report of ordinary social time. Later he can discuss the repeated messages as a relationship concern."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask a trusted person for perspective if this monitoring has become a pattern.", "consequence": "Kai describes the cancellations and repeated questions honestly. A trusted adult helps him consider whether his boundaries are being respected and how to respond safely. Jealous feelings can be discussed; pressure to become isolated or constantly monitored deserves support."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Send photos and live updates throughout the afternoon to prevent conflict.", "consequence": "Each update leads to another question. Kai misses much of the gathering and realizes reassurance has become surveillance. He can stop participating in that arrangement and seek support. Jules is responsible for handling jealousy without controlling Kai's friendships."}]}, {"title": "More than one place to belong", "text": ["Kai keeps his friendships and makes room for a conversation with Jules about the relationship. They may find a healthier agreement, or Kai may decide the pattern is not changing. Either way, the answer is not to make his world smaller until no one can feel jealous.", "Which choices offered care without giving up independence? What is the difference between a shared plan and a demand for proof? A relationship can be important without becoming someone's only source of connection."]}], "artCategory": "Relationships"}, {"id": "sports-bench", "title": "Beyond the Bench", "category": "Sports & clubs", "teaser": "Miles practiced all week. Watching the game from the bench was not the plan.", "skill": "Requesting feedback · Handling disappointment · Setting goals", "scenes": [{"title": "The warm-up ends", "text": ["Miles checks the scoreboard, then the coach. The second quarter ends without his name being called. He attended every practice this week and stayed late to work on defense. Across the court, his family waves. He looks at his shoes instead.", "Another substitute enters the game. Miles wants to ask what that player did that he did not. He knows the question will come out sharper than he intends. When the final whistle sounds, a teammate says, “Guess coach has favorites.” That explanation feels satisfying. It also gives Miles nothing useful to work on.", "He wants a fair chance and an honest explanation. He cannot choose the lineup, but he can choose how to ask about his place in it."]}, {"title": "After the handshake", "text": ["The coach is collecting equipment and talking to a parent. Miles is still upset. He wants answers tonight, but a crowded hallway is not the best place for a detailed conversation."], "question": "How does Miles ask for feedback?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Request a short private meeting before the next practice.", "consequence": "The coach agrees to ten minutes. Miles writes two questions before leaving: what limits his minutes, and what improvement would change that? Waiting feels uncomfortable, but it gives him a conversation rather than a public confrontation."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask one brief question now: “What should I focus on to earn more playing time?”", "consequence": "The coach names defensive positioning and offers to explain at practice. Miles asks for a specific example later. He has a starting point, not a complete answer, and avoids treating a rushed sentence as the whole evaluation."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Draft his questions tonight and send a respectful request tomorrow.", "consequence": "Writing helps Miles separate disappointment from accusations. He asks about his own role rather than comparing teammates. The next morning he still wants answers, but the message describes a goal the coach can respond to."}]}, {"title": "The example", "text": ["At practice, the coach shows a possession where Miles followed the ball and left his assignment open. Miles remembers making a strong play immediately afterward. Both moments happened. One good play does not erase the other, and one mistake does not define his ability."], "question": "What does he do with the explanation?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask to rehearse that situation and identify what a successful response looks like.", "consequence": "They run the position slowly before adding speed. Miles learns the exact movement the coach expects. He asks whether practice repetitions can be checked next week, turning “improve defense” into something observable."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Explain what he saw on the play and ask how to decide when to help.", "consequence": "The coach hears his reasoning and explains the team rule for switching assignments. Miles can question a decision without refusing instruction. They agree on a cue he can use when the situation becomes confusing."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Take notes, then ask for one priority instead of trying to change everything.", "consequence": "The coach chooses staying between his assignment and the basket. Miles repeats the instruction in his own words. Narrowing the focus makes practice manageable; it does not guarantee minutes or mean the other skills no longer matter."}]}, {"title": "Still waiting", "text": ["A week later, Miles has improved in drills but gets only two minutes. He feels the old frustration return. He wants to know whether the goalposts moved or whether progress and lineup decisions simply take different amounts of time."], "question": "How does he follow up?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask for a review of the agreed goal and an explanation of his current role.", "consequence": "The coach confirms progress and describes a specific substitute role. Miles asks what preparation that role needs. He may still dislike the minutes, but he has clearer information instead of guessing what silence means."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Request another observer’s feedback through the coach or assistant coach.", "consequence": "A second set of observations helps identify consistency under game pressure. Miles frames this as learning, not recruiting someone to take sides. The coaches agree on what to track so he is not chasing conflicting advice."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Discuss whether this team still fits his goals with a trusted adult, while honoring current commitments.", "consequence": "Miles compares development, enjoyment, and other options before deciding anything. He does not quit in the middle of a game to make a point. Considering a different path can be thoughtful rather than a punishment aimed at the coach."}]}, {"title": "A clearer goal", "text": ["Miles writes his next practice goal beside the date. He still wants more playing time. He also knows what question to ask and what evidence to look for. The bench no longer has to mean making up a story about his worth.", "Reflect: Which question produced useful information? How can someone disagree with a decision while still learning from feedback? Write a follow-up request that includes a specific goal rather than a comparison with another player."]}], "artCategory": "Sports & clubs"}, {"id": "sports-outside-circle", "title": "Outside the Circle", "category": "Sports & clubs", "teaser": "A captain keeps choosing the same friends. Leila wants a role, not a favor.", "skill": "Asking for inclusion · Using fair processes · Getting support", "scenes": [{"title": "The group photo", "text": ["Leila joins the debate club because she likes building an argument from evidence. For three meetings, she has organized notes while the captain assigns speaking roles to the same four friends. Tonight, the club posts a practice photo. Leila took it. She is not in it.", "The captain, Ben, says the competition team needs people who already work well together. Leila understands that experience matters. She also wonders how she is supposed to gain it if nobody lets her try.", "A friend tells her to stop showing up and see whether they notice. Leila would rather be heard before deciding whether to leave. She wants a fair opportunity, not a role awarded because someone feels guilty."]}, {"title": "Naming the request", "text": ["When practice ends, Leila catches Ben near the sign-in sheet. She has examples, but listing every slight could bury what she actually wants: a chance to demonstrate her skills."], "question": "What does she ask?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“Could I lead one practice argument next week so you can evaluate me?”", "consequence": "Ben agrees to a short practice slot. Leila confirms the topic and preparation expectations. It is a concrete opportunity, although not yet a competition place. She decides to check whether promised opportunities actually happen."}, {"label": "B", "text": "“What criteria decide speaking roles, and how can newer members meet them?”", "consequence": "Ben admits the process has been informal. Leila asks for clear criteria shared with everyone. The conversation shifts from whether he likes her to whether the club has a fair way to develop new speakers."}, {"label": "C", "text": "“I want a speaking role, but I keep getting support tasks. Can we discuss a rotation?”", "consequence": "Ben worries about preparation time. Leila proposes a practice rotation rather than immediately changing the competition lineup. They have room to negotiate because she identifies both the problem and a workable starting point."}]}, {"title": "Not just a joke", "text": ["At the next meeting, Ben says, “Everybody wants the spotlight now.” Some members laugh. Leila feels her request being turned into a personality problem. The faculty adviser is arranging papers nearby."], "question": "How does she respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I’m asking for practice experience, not special treatment,” then return to the task.", "consequence": "The response makes her position clear without insulting Ben. Leila later checks whether her practice slot remains on the agenda. A calm sentence helps, but follow-through matters more than winning the room for a moment."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask Ben privately to discuss requests without jokes at her expense.", "consequence": "He says he did not mean harm. Leila explains the effect and repeats what she needs going forward. She does not have to prove his intention to ask for a different way of handling disagreements."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ask the adviser for a private conversation about role access and the comment.", "consequence": "The adviser hears specific examples and asks what outcome Leila wants. She requests a transparent practice process, not automatic removal of the captain. The adviser can address club expectations without making Leila manage the problem alone."}]}, {"title": "A place on the agenda", "text": ["The adviser proposes open practice auditions with published criteria. Ben asks Leila to organize the signup sheet. She wants the new process to succeed, but she does not want administrative work to consume the preparation time she finally has."], "question": "What does she choose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Accept a limited organizing task with a clear handoff time and protected audition slot.", "consequence": "Leila helps for fifteen minutes, then another member takes over. She confirms her speaking time before agreeing. Helping the club and protecting her opportunity become compatible because the boundary is specific."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decline organizing this time and explain that she needs to prepare.", "consequence": "Another member handles the sheet. Leila feels awkward saying no, but the club still functions. Declining one task is not refusing to contribute; it makes room for the role she has consistently requested."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Suggest sharing administrative tasks among members, including experienced speakers.", "consequence": "The adviser sets a rotation. It may take adjustment, but preparation and support work become visible responsibilities instead of jobs assigned to whoever stays quiet. Leila still completes her own part of the rotation."}]}, {"title": "Her own voice", "text": ["Leila’s audition is not perfect. She loses her place once, then finds it. The evaluation includes strengths and a skill to develop. For the first time, feedback concerns her actual speaking instead of whether she has been around long enough.", "Reflect: Which request made fairness easier to evaluate? Explain the difference between asking for a guaranteed outcome and asking for a genuine opportunity. Write a boundary that lets someone contribute without always being pushed into the same role."]}], "artCategory": "Sports & clubs"}, {"id": "sports-too-many-tabs", "title": "Too Many Tabs", "category": "Sports & clubs", "teaser": "The club showcase depends on Imani. At least, that is what everyone keeps telling her.", "skill": "Managing workload · Sharing responsibility · Communicating limits", "scenes": [{"title": "The dependable one", "text": ["Imani has seven tabs open: ticket orders, music, a rehearsal calendar, the poster, a budget, a group chat, and unfinished homework. She joined the arts club to perform. Somewhere between being organized and saying yes, she became responsible for almost everything except her own rehearsal.", "The showcase is next Friday. A message arrives asking her to arrange refreshments too. “You’re the only one who actually gets things done,” the vice president writes. The compliment lands like another assignment.", "Imani wants the event to succeed. She also wants sleep, time for schoolwork, and the performance she signed up for. If she waits until she snaps, nobody will know which jobs need another owner."]}, {"title": "The next yes", "text": ["Imani starts typing “Fine,” then stops. Being capable does not create extra hours. She needs to describe the workload before agreeing to add anything else."], "question": "How does she respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "List her current tasks and ask the club to choose two priorities for her.", "consequence": "The list surprises members who saw only their individual requests. They identify tickets and the budget as her priorities. The remaining work still needs owners, so Imani asks for names and dates rather than accepting vague offers."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decline refreshments and offer to hand over the supplier information.", "consequence": "Another member takes the task. Imani shares the information she already has without taking responsibility for every follow-up. A useful handoff lets her help without quietly becoming the backup owner again."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Request a brief planning meeting with the adviser before accepting more work.", "consequence": "The adviser schedules a check-in and asks everyone to bring task lists. Imani has not accused the club of laziness. She has identified a capacity problem early enough for the group to change the plan."}]}, {"title": "The guilt line", "text": ["At the meeting, a friend says, “If you drop stuff now, the whole event could fall apart.” Imani hears the fear underneath the pressure. She does not want that fear to decide how much she must carry."], "question": "What does she say?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I’m telling you now so we can fix it. I can finish these two jobs, not all seven.”", "consequence": "The adviser helps assign the other tasks. Some work is simplified. Imani keeps her commitments specific, allowing the group to plan around reality instead of counting on a last-minute rescue."}, {"label": "B", "text": "“Let’s decide what we can cut if nobody can own it.”", "consequence": "The club removes an elaborate photo display and chooses simpler refreshments. Members are disappointed, but a smaller complete event is more realistic than an ambitious plan held together by one exhausted person."}, {"label": "C", "text": "“I need rehearsal time too. Can we build a schedule that protects it?”", "consequence": "The club puts rehearsal blocks on the same calendar as setup tasks. Naming her original goal helps members see that overload costs more than convenience. Imani agrees to use the protected time rather than filling it with hidden work."}]}, {"title": "The missing poster", "text": ["Two days before the showcase, the new poster owner has not finished. Someone asks Imani to take it back. She could do it, but only by missing rehearsal again. The deadline matters; so does whether shared responsibility means anything."], "question": "How does she handle the deadline?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Offer a short troubleshooting call while the assigned member completes the poster.", "consequence": "They solve a formatting problem together. Imani stops at the agreed time and goes to rehearsal. Support does not become a silent transfer of ownership, and the other member gains experience finishing the task."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the adviser to choose between a simpler poster and another available volunteer.", "consequence": "The adviser approves a basic version. It lacks the original design flourishes but includes the needed information. Imani does not have to prove commitment by restoring every feature the club hoped to include."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Take the poster only if another member formally takes one of her remaining tasks.", "consequence": "They agree on a real exchange with deadlines and access to the files. Imani checks that the replacement has what they need. Flexibility works because the swap reduces her load instead of merely adding another emergency."}]}, {"title": "Under the lights", "text": ["Imani reaches the stage with time to breathe. The showcase has imperfections, but it happens because several people finish their parts. Afterward, she suggests keeping named task owners and protected rehearsal blocks for the next event.", "Reflect: When did a compliment become pressure? Write a response that states capacity without apologizing for having limits. How can a team distinguish helping someone from taking over their responsibility?"]}], "artCategory": "Sports & clubs"}, {"id": "sports-final-play", "title": "The Final Play", "category": "Sports & clubs", "teaser": "One mistake is becoming the explanation for an entire loss. Rafael knows the game was bigger than that.", "skill": "Handling blame · Repairing mistakes · Team communication", "scenes": [{"title": "The ride home", "text": ["Rafael’s pass was intercepted with twelve seconds left. The other team scored. In the locker room, nobody says much until a teammate mutters, “We had it until that pass.” Rafael hears every word.", "He knows the pass was risky. He also remembers missed free throws, late rotations, and an early lead disappearing. He wants to name every teammate’s mistake until the room understands that he was not the whole game. He knows that would turn the loss into a list of accusations.", "On the ride home, the team chat starts replaying the final seconds. Rafael wants accountability without becoming a target. He needs a way to own his decision without accepting everyone else’s anger as his responsibility."]}, {"title": "What to own", "text": ["The chat asks why he threw the pass. Rafael could explain what he saw, but several messages are arriving at once. He does not need to answer every comment separately."], "question": "What does he send?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“I thought the lane was open. I got it wrong. I’ll review it with coach tomorrow.”", "consequence": "The message acknowledges the decision without calling himself useless. Rafael mutes the conversation for the evening. He has committed to learning, not to spending the night accepting repeated criticism from an audience."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask to discuss the play during the team review rather than in a heated chat.", "consequence": "Some teammates want an immediate explanation. Rafael repeats that he is willing to discuss it with context. Moving the conversation does not erase accountability; it gives the group a better setting to examine the play."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Contact the captain privately and ask for help keeping the chat focused on team improvement.", "consequence": "The captain asks teammates to stop piling on and save analysis for practice. Rafael still prepares to explain his choice. Seeking support changes the tone of the conversation without requiring someone else to excuse the mistake."}]}, {"title": "Watching it again", "text": ["During film review, the coach pauses before the pass. Rafael sees the defender moving earlier than he remembered. Then a teammate says, “Exactly. You lost it for us.” The coach begins to redirect, and Rafael has a chance to speak."], "question": "How does Rafael respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "“That was my mistake. Saying I lost the whole game isn’t useful. What read should I make here?”", "consequence": "The coach explains a safer option. Rafael protects the boundary while keeping the lesson in view. The team can analyze the decision without turning a single player into the explanation for every possession."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the coach to review the final few possessions, including his own, as a team sequence.", "consequence": "The wider review shows several decisions that shaped the ending. Rafael does not use those mistakes to cancel his. The team discusses patterns and responsibilities instead of assigning the entire result to one moment."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Let the coach redirect, then ask for a private conversation about the repeated blame.", "consequence": "Rafael chooses not to argue in the room. After practice, he describes the comments and asks how the coach will handle them. Silence in the moment is not agreement when he follows up through a safer channel."}]}, {"title": "The next close game", "text": ["A few days later, the score is tied. The same teammate says, “Don’t throw it away this time.” Rafael feels himself getting tight. He needs to stay involved, not disappear from every decision because he fears another mistake."], "question": "What does he do?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Use a brief boundary: “Talk about this play, not last game,” then confirm the plan.", "consequence": "The teammate gives the current assignment. Rafael focuses on the next read. He cannot guarantee a perfect result, but he can ask for communication that helps the team perform instead of replaying an old accusation."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the coach for a quick clarification of his role before the restart.", "consequence": "The coach gives a clear option and backup. Asking a tactical question helps Rafael reenter the game. He is not demanding protection from every difficult moment; he is getting information he can act on."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Use the team’s agreed reset cue and address the comment afterward with support.", "consequence": "Rafael takes a breath and returns to the present possession. Later, he reports that the comments continued. A reset helps with immediate focus, but it does not replace addressing a repeated pattern of disrespect."}]}, {"title": "More than twelve seconds", "text": ["The next result does not rewrite the previous loss. Rafael still made the pass. He has also reviewed it, practiced a different read, and asked teammates to communicate differently. Responsibility has become a next step rather than a permanent label.", "Reflect: What is the difference between owning a mistake and accepting blame for everything? Write useful feedback a teammate could give. Explain when repeated comments need help from a coach or another trusted adult."]}], "artCategory": "Sports & clubs"}, {"id": "sports-two-commitments", "title": "Two Commitments", "category": "Sports & clubs", "teaser": "The robotics final and a newly posted work shift land on the same afternoon. Neither team wants to hear no.", "skill": "Planning · Negotiating schedules · Confirming agreements", "scenes": [{"title": "Two calendars", "text": ["Tessa checks the work schedule while waiting for robotics club. Saturday, noon to six. The regional final begins at one. She requested that afternoon off two weeks ago, but she cannot find a reply confirming approval.", "Her teammates count on her to run the presentation. Her manager counts on a full weekend crew. She needs the wages and cares about the competition. A teammate says the job should understand. A coworker says clubs are optional. Neither comment makes the dates stop overlapping.", "Tessa opens her request and reads it carefully. She sent it, but sending and approval are not the same thing. There is still time to ask questions before either group is left waiting for someone who cannot arrive."]}, {"title": "Start with the facts", "text": ["Tessa does not know whether the manager missed the request or could not grant it. She needs an answer before promising the club she will definitely be there."], "question": "How does she begin?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Show the manager the dated request and ask whether it was approved or overlooked.", "consequence": "The manager finds the message but no approval. Tessa explains the competition timing and asks what options remain. Keeping the discussion factual helps solve the schedule without arguing about whose activity matters more."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask about the workplace’s approved shift-swap process and available coverage.", "consequence": "A coworker may be willing to trade, but Tessa learns the manager must confirm it. She does not treat a friendly text as a completed change. She also checks whether the trade would create a new conflict."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell the club adviser the uncertainty and ask for a backup presentation plan while she checks work.", "consequence": "The adviser begins preparing another speaker without removing Tessa from the team. Sharing the problem early reduces risk. Tessa gives a specific time for an update instead of leaving everyone guessing until Saturday."}]}, {"title": "Pressure from both sides", "text": ["A teammate messages, “If you cared, you’d just call out.” At work, a coworker says, “Don’t make the rest of us cover your hobby.” Tessa feels pushed to defend her whole character instead of discussing one afternoon."], "question": "How does she set a boundary?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Tell both groups she is arranging a legitimate solution and will update them by tomorrow afternoon.", "consequence": "The deadline gives people something concrete to plan around. Tessa does not promise the outcome they prefer. She rejects the idea that caring means making a commitment she cannot actually keep."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask the adviser and manager separately for the minimum coverage each role needs.", "consequence": "The club needs a presentation handoff; the store needs the busy hours covered. Specific needs reveal possible compromises. Tessa asks questions without assuming either adult can or must solve the entire conflict for her."}, {"label": "C", "text": "State that guilt comments are not helping, then return to the available options.", "consequence": "Some people remain annoyed. Tessa keeps the discussion about coverage and timing rather than trading insults. Setting the boundary does not guarantee approval, but it protects her ability to think through the choices."}]}, {"title": "A real choice", "text": ["The manager offers a shorter shift ending at two, but the competition venue is forty minutes away and her team presents at one. A coworker can cover the entire shift only if Tessa takes a Sunday shift. The club can also use its backup speaker."], "question": "Which workable plan does she choose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Take the Sunday trade after checking her other commitments and obtaining manager approval.", "consequence": "Tessa confirms the swap in writing and tells the club she can attend. She gives up part of Sunday, a real cost rather than a magical solution. Everyone has the same final schedule before Saturday."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Keep the work shift and prepare the backup speaker with notes and a rehearsal.", "consequence": "Missing the final hurts, but Tessa remains a contributing teammate. She explains the confirmed decision promptly. The club can be disappointed without being abandoned, because the handoff happens before the event."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Accept the shorter shift and attend the later part of the final, leaving the presentation to the backup.", "consequence": "Tessa checks transportation and confirms exactly when she can arrive. She does not claim she can make the one o’clock presentation. The compromise supports both commitments while acknowledging that she will miss an important part."}]}, {"title": "Confirmed, not assumed", "text": ["Tessa puts the final plan on both calendars. For the next competition, she sets an earlier reminder to request time off and another to check approval. The lesson is not that one commitment always wins. It is that people need accurate information before they can depend on a plan.", "Reflect: Which detail prevented an unrealistic promise? Write a schedule-change request with a date, time, and clear question. How can someone communicate disappointment without accusing another person of not caring?"]}], "artCategory": "Sports & clubs"}, {"id": "more-life-quiet", "title": "The Quietest Corner", "category": "Home life", "teaser": "The exam is tomorrow. Every room is loud. Asking for quiet feels like asking everyone else to disappear.", "skill": "Naming needs and sharing space", "artCategory": "Home life", "scenes": [{"title": "A full house", "text": ["Tessa spread her notes across the kitchen table, the only surface large enough for her books. Tomorrow’s certification quiz could qualify her for the next workshop at school. She wanted this opportunity, and she needed more than a quick glance at the material."]}, {"title": "The first interruption", "text": ["When a child reached for her highlighter, Tessa pulled her papers away. “Can everybody just stop?” she said. Her aunt looked up. “People live here, Tessa.” Tessa heard a dismissal, although her aunt might have heard an order to clear the kitchen.", "She could explain the actual problem instead of debating who had a right to be there. She needed a quiet interval, not ownership of the whole house. The quiz covered procedures she had to understand, and she was already losing track of the steps."], "question": "How can Tessa make her need clear?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask for a specific quiet period and explain what she needs to finish.", "consequence": "Tessa asks whether the table can be quieter from seven to seven-thirty. Her aunt says dinner will still be happening then, but a later window might work. A specific request gives the family something practical to discuss, even though the first proposed time is unavailable."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask whether another space could be available temporarily.", "consequence": "Her aunt suggests the small bedroom after a phone call ends. Tessa checks the lighting and whether she can bring a chair. The room is not ideal, but asking about alternatives finds an option that does not require everyone else to stop their evening."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say nothing else and keep working through the noise.", "consequence": "Tessa reads the same paragraph several times and realizes she is guessing at answers. Staying quiet avoids another tense moment but does not meet her study need. She can still explain that she needs a workable plan rather than pretending the current arrangement is enough."}]}, {"title": "A plan with limits", "text": ["The family discusses a half-hour in the bedroom after dinner. Tessa also needs her brother’s speaker turned down near that room. He says headphones bother his ears and asks why her quiz should change his whole evening.", "Tessa does not know how to solve his discomfort for him. She can describe the effect of the sound and offer a limited compromise without deciding that her needs matter more than his. Her aunt reminds them that a short agreement is different from a new permanent household rule."], "question": "What should Tessa propose?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask him to use a lower volume for the agreed half-hour.", "consequence": "He agrees to lower the speaker and move it farther from the door. Tessa tells him when she expects to finish. A time limit makes the request easier to understand. They agree she will ask again if the sound still carries rather than banging on the wall."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Offer to swap quiet time for helping him with a task afterward.", "consequence": "Her brother accepts the offer, but Tessa checks that the task will take only ten minutes. Trading help can be fair when both people freely agree. She does not promise an entire evening of chores just to earn a basic chance to study."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell him he clearly does not care whether she succeeds.", "consequence": "Her brother starts defending himself instead of discussing volume. Tessa notices the conversation has shifted from sound to character. She restarts: “I’m stressed. I need thirty minutes with less noise.” Naming the practical need gives them another way forward."}]}, {"title": "The plan changes", "text": ["Ten minutes into studying, a relative needs to use the bedroom. Tessa feels the familiar rush of frustration: she finally settled down, and now she has to move again. Her aunt offers the kitchen after everyone has finished eating.", "The original agreement cannot be followed exactly. Tessa still has choices. She can identify what she has already completed, what remains, and whether a short change in method would help. She does not have to pretend the interruption is fine to respond without an argument."], "question": "What is a useful next step?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain what remains and request a new uninterrupted window.", "consequence": "Tessa says she has two procedures left and needs twenty minutes. Her aunt clears one end of the table and asks the family to use the living room briefly. The revised plan is specific, and Tessa sets a timer so everyone knows when the arrangement ends."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask someone to quiz her while the quiet space is unavailable.", "consequence": "Her cousin agrees to read the practice questions aloud. This is not the same as silent reading, but it helps Tessa find the steps she does not understand. When the table is free, she uses the remaining time for those gaps instead of starting over."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Close the books and decide the evening is ruined.", "consequence": "Taking a break lowers Tessa’s frustration, but abandoning the review would leave her less prepared. After a few minutes, she chooses one essential section to finish. A disrupted plan does not require an all-or-nothing response; a smaller useful step still counts."}]}, {"title": "Making room", "text": ["Tessa completes a focused review and writes down one question to ask her instructor. She cannot guarantee her quiz result. She can recognize that she asked for conditions that helped her learn and adjusted when the first plan changed."]}]}, {"id": "more-life-paycheck", "title": "The Missing Hours", "category": "Work life", "teaser": "The first paycheck should feel exciting. The hours on it do not match the hours you remember working.", "skill": "Checking facts and requesting correction", "artCategory": "Work life", "scenes": [{"title": "A number that does not fit", "text": ["Andre opened his first pay statement outside the grocery store. He had worked two busy weekends and several short shifts after school. He expected deductions, but the listed hours were lower than the total he had written in his planner."]}, {"title": "Before the accusation", "text": ["Andre checked his planner and found one shift was outside the dates printed on the statement. That explained part of the difference, but three hours still appeared to be missing. He remembered staying late to help unload a delivery.", "His supervisor, Ms. Chen, was answering questions at the service desk. Andre could raise the issue, but a line of customers stood nearby. He wanted a clear answer rather than a rushed argument about whether he remembered correctly."], "question": "How should Andre begin?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask for a private time to compare the pay period and his shift record.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen offers ten minutes after the customer line clears. Andre brings the statement and his dated notes. Asking to compare records does not weaken his concern. It makes the discussion about a specific discrepancy rather than a conclusion neither person has checked."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask who handles time records and what information they need.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen explains that payroll reviews corrections and gives Andre the workplace process. He writes down the contact and required dates. Finding the right person can be an effective first step, especially when the supervisor cannot directly change a pay statement."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Announce that the store has stolen his wages.", "consequence": "Several people turn around, and Ms. Chen asks to discuss the concern away from customers. Andre’s concern still deserves attention, but the accusation has started a separate conflict. He restates the facts: the statement and his notes differ by three hours, and he wants them reviewed."}]}, {"title": "An incomplete record", "text": ["The time system shows a clock-out at the end of Andre’s scheduled shift. His planner says he stayed for the delivery. Ms. Chen says he may have clocked out before helping, and asks whether he remembers who was supervising the unloading.", "Andre names the supervisor who asked him to stay. He does not have to invent a perfect memory or apologize for asking about his pay. He needs to separate what he knows from what the store should check in its records."], "question": "What information should Andre provide?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Give the date, approximate additional time, task, and supervisor involved.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen records those details and says she will compare them with the delivery and staffing records. Andre labels the time as approximate instead of pretending he knows the exact minute. Clear, honest information supports a review without requiring him to prove every detail immediately."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for a copy or view of the time record and keep his own notes.", "consequence": "They review the entry together. Andre writes down the displayed times and asks how to submit a correction request. Keeping his records helps him follow the issue over time. He does not secretly access anyone else’s account or alter the system himself."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Add extra time to his estimate because the store caused the problem.", "consequence": "Andre starts to round the three hours upward, then realizes an inflated claim would make the record less reliable. He corrects the estimate before submitting it. Requesting fair pay means reporting the time he actually believes he worked, not adding a penalty of his own."}]}, {"title": "Not today", "text": ["Ms. Chen says the review cannot be completed before Andre leaves. Andre worries that “we’ll check” means nothing will happen. He has heard that answer before in other situations, and he does not want the concern to disappear.", "A useful next step needs an owner and a time to check back. Andre can ask for those without demanding that a person at the service desk solve every payroll issue instantly. If the agreed process does not work, he can seek further appropriate support."], "question": "How can he follow through?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask who will review it, when to expect an update, and how to follow up.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen identifies payroll as the reviewer and gives a specific day for an update. Andre repeats the plan and notes it. A date is not a guarantee of the outcome, but it gives him a clear next step instead of repeatedly asking different people the same question."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Send a brief factual written summary through the workplace’s approved channel.", "consequence": "Andre lists the date, discrepancy, and requested review, without accusations or unrelated personal details. The written summary helps everyone refer to the same issue. He keeps a copy and asks a trusted adult for help understanding the response if the process becomes confusing."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decide asking again will make him seem difficult and drop it.", "consequence": "Andre nearly leaves without a follow-up plan. Then he remembers the missing money affects his bus costs. He asks one practical question before going: “When should I check back?” Respectful persistence is different from creating trouble, and his pay concern is worth following through."}]}, {"title": "An answer in writing", "text": ["Payroll confirms an adjustment is needed and explains when it will appear. Andre compares the next statement rather than assuming the matter is finished. He also learns how to record an approved late finish correctly in the future."]}]}, {"id": "more-life-expensive", "title": "The Price of Going", "category": "Friendships", "teaser": "Everyone says the weekend will be unforgettable. Nobody seems to notice what it costs.", "skill": "Financial boundaries without personal disclosure", "artCategory": "Friendships", "scenes": [{"title": "The group plan", "text": ["Imani’s friends had a plan for Saturday: an escape room, dinner, and a ride-share home. The group chat filled with excited messages. Imani wanted to celebrate her friend’s birthday, but the total was more than she could comfortably spend.", "She had money saved for something important. She did not want to explain her family’s budget or turn a birthday conversation into a debate about whether her reason was good enough. She also did not want to disappear without saying anything."]}, {"title": "Just this once", "text": ["“Come on, it’s one night,” her friend Rosa wrote. Another friend offered to cover Imani, saying she could pay it back whenever. The offer sounded generous, but owing someone would make Imani anxious. She had not agreed to borrow money.", "Imani could value the friendship and still have a limit. She needed to communicate the limit clearly enough that the group could plan, without sharing private details to earn permission."], "question": "What could Imani say?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "State what she can spend and ask about joining part of the evening.", "consequence": "Imani says she can join dinner if they choose a place within her limit. Rosa checks the menu instead of guessing. A clear number helps with planning, but Imani can also choose not to share an exact budget and simply name the activity she can afford."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decline the loan and suggest celebrating together another day.", "consequence": "Imani thanks her friend for offering but says borrowing is not comfortable for her. She proposes making a birthday playlist and meeting at the park Sunday. The alternative shows interest without treating debt as the price of keeping the friendship."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Agree now and hope she will find the money before Saturday.", "consequence": "The group includes her in the booking count. Imani feels relieved for a moment, then more worried. Before anyone pays a nonrefundable cost for her, she corrects her answer. Saying yes too quickly does not prevent her from being honest while plans can still change."}]}, {"title": "The joke", "text": ["Someone replies, “Okay, budget queen.” Imani cannot tell whether it is meant affectionately. She knows it makes her uncomfortable. Rosa says the group is only joking, and Imani feels pressure to laugh so she will not seem sensitive.", "She does not have to prove the joke was intended to hurt her. She can describe its effect and request a change. She can also choose a private conversation if the group chat feels like an audience."], "question": "How should she handle the comment?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask the group not to make jokes about her spending limit.", "consequence": "Imani writes, “I know you may mean it as a joke, but I don’t like comments about my money. Please leave that out.” One friend apologizes; another says nothing. The request is clear even without unanimous agreement or a perfect apology."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Message Rosa privately and explain that the joke made it harder to speak up.", "consequence": "Rosa says she had not noticed how the comment landed. Imani asks her not to dismiss it as harmless if it happens again. A private conversation lets them focus on the friendship, although Imani may still need a direct boundary in the group later."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Make a harsh joke about someone else’s spending.", "consequence": "The conversation turns into competing insults. Imani pauses and removes her own insulting reply where possible, then says what she actually needs. She can take responsibility for her response without deciding the original joke was acceptable."}]}, {"title": "The changed price", "text": ["By Friday, the group finds a cheaper activity, but the shared ride still costs more than Imani expected. She has already explained herself twice and feels tired of being the person who complicates everything.", "Her original limit has not changed just because the group made an effort. She appreciates that effort and checks the remaining details. A plan is only useful if the real total, including transportation, works."], "question": "What should she do before confirming?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Check the total and confirm only the part that fits her limit.", "consequence": "Imani calculates the activity, food, and ride together. She chooses dinner only and arranges a safe ride with someone she trusts. She tells the group before they book. Clear limits prevent a surprise bill from becoming a bigger conflict afterward."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decline Saturday and keep the separate celebration plan.", "consequence": "Imani says she appreciates the changes but Saturday still will not work. Rosa is disappointed, but they keep Sunday’s plan. Disappointment is not proof that Imani has done something wrong; friends can care about each other without sharing every experience."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Use her savings because everyone has already adjusted for her.", "consequence": "Imani considers whether the tradeoff is genuinely worth it to her, not just whether she feels guilty. She realizes it would delay her own goal. She declines before committing. Someone else’s effort deserves thanks, but it does not create a debt of agreement."}]}, {"title": "Still part of the group", "text": ["The birthday happens, and Imani joins the celebration in a way she can manage. Some friends understand immediately; others need reminders. She notices that naming a limit felt awkward, but hiding it would not have made the cost disappear."]}]}, {"id": "more-life-ride", "title": "The Ride That Fell Through", "category": "Summer plans", "teaser": "Your first shift starts in an hour. The person who promised a ride has just canceled.", "skill": "Planning, timely communication, and safe alternatives", "artCategory": "Summer plans", "scenes": [{"title": "Forty-five minutes", "text": ["Nolan had laid out his work shirt the night before. His first shift at the community garden store started at nine. At eight-fifteen, his cousin called: the car would not start, and the promised ride was gone."]}, {"title": "Who needs to know?", "text": ["The store was too far to walk safely before the shift. A bus might get him close, although Nolan had never used that route. His manager’s contact information was in the welcome message. Nolan worried that calling would sound like making an excuse.", "He could communicate a problem without claiming it was solved. An early factual update would give the manager more information than silence. He needed to avoid promising an arrival time he had not checked."], "question": "What should Nolan do first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Contact the manager now, explain briefly, and say he is checking safe alternatives.", "consequence": "The manager appreciates the early notice and asks Nolan to update her when he knows more. She cannot promise there will be no effect on the shift, but they can plan. Nolan has not solved transportation yet; he has prevented the store from having to guess where he is."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Check one realistic transport option quickly, then call with the verified timing.", "consequence": "Nolan checks the bus timetable and discovers the first route would arrive after nine. He calls promptly with that information rather than continuing an endless search. A short fact check helps; waiting until every possibility is exhausted would leave the manager uninformed."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Wait until nine because he might still find a ride.", "consequence": "Nolan spends several minutes messaging people without an answer. He realizes waiting is making the communication problem worse. He contacts the manager before the shift begins, explains what is known, and avoids blaming his cousin in a long story."}]}, {"title": "A tempting offer", "text": ["Someone Nolan barely knows replies in a neighborhood chat and offers a ride. The person asks him to meet at a place away from his home. Nolan feels pressure to accept because the offer could get him there on time.", "He also has a possible later ride from a trusted family friend and a bus option he can verify. Arriving punctually matters, but it does not erase the need to make safe transportation choices. Nolan can ask a trusted adult for help assessing options."], "question": "Which next step protects both safety and the work plan?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Decline the unfamiliar ride and confirm the trusted option’s timing.", "consequence": "The family friend can take him at nine-fifteen. Nolan confirms the destination and pickup details, then tells the manager his realistic arrival time. He may still be late, but he is not exchanging safety for the appearance of reliability."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Verify the bus route and stop with a trusted adult before deciding.", "consequence": "They check that the stop is accessible and the route is operating. Nolan confirms how he will get home too. Public transport becomes a workable option because he has checked details, not because he assumes every route or stop will be suitable."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Accept the unfamiliar offer without checking because he cannot disappoint the manager.", "consequence": "Nolan starts to agree, then notices he cannot verify the person or plan. He stops and asks a trusted adult for help. Work expectations do not require accepting a ride that feels unsafe. He can choose a safer delay and communicate it honestly."}]}, {"title": "The next shift", "text": ["The manager agrees to a later arrival today but asks about tomorrow. Nolan feels defensive: the car failure was not his fault. Still, tomorrow’s transportation needs a plan that does not depend on the same broken car suddenly being repaired.", "He can acknowledge the impact without accepting blame for causing the breakdown. A backup plan should be realistic, confirmed, and within his resources. “I’ll figure it out” is a hope, not a schedule."], "question": "What should Nolan arrange?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Confirm a primary ride and a backup, including how to contact work if either changes.", "consequence": "Nolan checks availability instead of assuming someone will help. He writes down the bus details as a backup and saves the store contact. The plan does not prevent every disruption, but it gives him a sequence of actions before a problem becomes an emergency."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask whether a temporary start-time adjustment is possible while transport is repaired.", "consequence": "The manager considers the staffing needs and explains which shift times have flexibility. Nolan understands that asking does not guarantee approval. They agree on a short-term arrangement with a date to review it, rather than leaving the schedule uncertain."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Promise he will never be late again.", "consequence": "The promise sounds confident but covers events Nolan cannot control. He replaces it with a concrete commitment: confirm transportation tonight and notify the store promptly if anything changes. Reliability is better supported by a plan than an absolute promise."}]}, {"title": "Showing up prepared", "text": ["Nolan arrives, checks in, and begins the training he can still complete. The morning was not what he planned, but the manager knows what happened and what he is doing next. Nolan does not need to repeat a long apology throughout the day."]}]}, {"id": "more-life-holiday", "title": "Two Places at Once", "category": "Christmas break", "teaser": "You promised to help at the holiday drive. Your family has made a plan for the same afternoon.", "skill": "Balancing commitments and communicating changes", "artCategory": "Christmas break", "scenes": [{"title": "The overlapping plans", "text": ["Leah signed up to help pack food boxes at the community center on the first Saturday of Christmas break. She wanted volunteer experience and had promised her friend she would be there. Then her father announced a family visit that afternoon.", "He had assumed she was free because school was out. Leah had assumed the family knew about the volunteer shift because she mentioned it once during dinner. Neither assumption put the two plans on the same calendar."]}, {"title": "Whose plan counts?", "text": ["“You can volunteer another time,” her father said. Leah heard that her commitment did not matter. She almost replied that he always ruined her plans. The visit mattered too: a relative was only in town for one day.", "Leah needed to explain what she had already agreed to and learn which parts of the family plan were flexible. She did not have to dismiss the visit to ask that her own commitment be considered."], "question": "How should Leah start the conversation?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Explain the existing commitment and ask to compare the times.", "consequence": "Leah shows the volunteer message and asks when the family needs to leave. Her father realizes she is assigned to a specific task, not simply planning to drop in. They discover part of the afternoon may overlap while the morning remains open."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask for a short planning conversation when neither person is rushing.", "consequence": "They agree to talk after breakfast. Leah writes down the shift time and the questions she needs answered. Choosing a calmer moment helps them discuss options, provided she does not postpone so long that the organizers cannot adjust their plans."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell her father she has already decided and refuse to discuss it.", "consequence": "Her father focuses on the refusal rather than the schedule. Leah restarts by stating what she committed to and asking what cannot change about the visit. A firm need can be expressed without treating everyone else’s plans as irrelevant."}]}, {"title": "A possible compromise", "text": ["The family could leave later, but that would shorten the visit. Leah could ask the volunteer coordinator about an earlier task, but someone else might need to cover her original station. She does not know yet whether either change is possible.", "A compromise cannot depend on silently handing work to someone else. Leah needs to ask, not announce, and give people a chance to respond. She can offer useful alternatives while being honest about the conflict."], "question": "What should Leah ask the coordinator?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask whether she can work an earlier shift or complete another needed task.", "consequence": "The coordinator checks the plan and finds boxes that need labeling before the drive opens. Leah confirms the task and finish time. The earlier work is genuinely useful; it is not a pretend replacement invented only to make the conflict disappear."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Explain the conflict and ask what coverage would be needed if she leaves early.", "consequence": "The coordinator says the station needs two people until one. Leah asks whether another volunteer can switch with her, without assuming her friend must do it. She waits for confirmation before telling her family the problem is solved."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say she is sick so nobody will question her absence.", "consequence": "Leah considers the excuse, then realizes it would hide a solvable scheduling issue and damage trust if discovered. She sends a truthful, brief explanation instead. She does not need to share private family details to communicate that a conflict has come up."}]}, {"title": "The friend’s reaction", "text": ["Her friend Mateo says, “So I’m doing your work now?” Leah feels guilty and irritated. She has not asked him to cover, and the coordinator is handling the schedule. Still, Mateo expected them to volunteer together.", "Leah can acknowledge his disappointment without accepting an inaccurate description of the plan. She needs to distinguish the friendship conversation from the practical staffing agreement. Both matter, but they are not the same problem."], "question": "How can Leah respond?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Acknowledge the changed plan and explain the confirmed coverage.", "consequence": "Leah says she is sorry their shared afternoon changed, then names the task she will complete and the coordinator’s coverage plan. Mateo is still disappointed, but he understands he has not automatically inherited her shift. Clear information reduces the room for assumptions."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what Mateo is worried will happen and discuss that specific concern.", "consequence": "Mateo admits he is nervous about running the station with someone new. Leah suggests he ask the coordinator for a quick introduction and task review. She can support him without promising to be in two places at once or managing the staffing herself."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Accuse Mateo of being selfish for not understanding.", "consequence": "Mateo pulls back, and the conversation becomes about who is the better friend. Leah pauses and says she wants to explain, not attack him. She can own the insult while keeping the revised commitment. Repair does not require undoing every reasonable boundary."}]}, {"title": "A plan people can use", "text": ["Leah completes the confirmed volunteer task and joins the family visit. She checks out with the coordinator rather than leaving without a word. Later, she asks Mateo how his shift went and listens without demanding that he say everything was fine."]}]}, {"id": "more-school-noise", "title": "Too Much in the Room", "category": "School drama", "teaser": "The test has started. Every sound feels louder than the question in front of you.", "skill": "Requesting a workable learning environment", "artCategory": "School drama", "scenes": [{"title": "A room that will not settle", "text": ["Riley knows the material. They reviewed the practice problems twice and explained one to a friend before class. Now a cart rattles outside the door, someone taps a pencil, and students from another class are arriving late. Riley has reread question one without taking in a word.", "The teacher, Ms. Chen, says there are thirty minutes left. Riley feels embarrassed by how quickly everyone else seems to be working. They have never discussed a formal support plan with her. They do not know what changes are available today, but staying silent will not make the room quieter."]}, {"title": "The first request", "text": ["Riley raises a hand. Ms. Chen walks over, but a student nearby looks up. Riley wants help, not an announcement about their difficulty. The request needs enough detail to explain the barrier without sharing more than they want.", "A quieter space might help. So might moving away from the door or taking a brief pause. Riley cannot know which option is available without asking."], "question": "How should Riley start?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask quietly for a different seat and explain that the noise is interrupting concentration.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen offers a seat away from the door. Riley has named a specific problem and a practical option, not asked her to guess. It may help, though they will need to check whether the change is enough."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask to speak at the doorway before explaining the difficulty.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen checks that the class can continue, then steps over with Riley. The conversation is more private. Riley explains what is happening and asks which testing options are available without assuming a separate room is guaranteed."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Keep working and hope the problem disappears.", "consequence": "Several minutes pass with little progress. Riley realizes endurance is not the same as access. They can still ask now: ‘I tried to continue, but the noise is stopping me from concentrating. What can we change?’"}]}, {"title": "An option, not a complete solution", "text": ["Ms. Chen explains that the supervised room used for some tests is occupied today. She can move Riley and check in after a few minutes. She can also discuss what to do about time lost while solving the problem.", "Riley is disappointed. Asking for support did not produce the exact answer they pictured. They need to decide whether the available option is worth trying and how to describe what is still difficult."], "question": "What should Riley do with the available option?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Try the quieter seat and agree on a short check-in.", "consequence": "They agree to check after five minutes. Riley completes two questions, which gives them useful information about what helps. The trial does not require Riley to pretend the problem is completely solved."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what other supervised options could be arranged, even if not immediately.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen explains who coordinates testing spaces. They make a plan for a conversation after class while Riley tries the best available seat. Riley learns the next step instead of being left with a vague ‘maybe later.’"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say the whole test is pointless and refuse every alternative.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen hears the frustration but cannot tell which adjustment Riley needs. Riley resets: ‘I am worried I cannot show what I know. Can we agree on one option to try and review the time I lost?’"}]}, {"title": "Planning beyond today", "text": ["The room settles, and Riley answers more questions. They still worry about being judged by work completed under conditions that made concentration difficult. Ms. Chen offers to review what happened after class rather than debate the score in front of everyone.", "Riley wants future tests to start differently. They can describe the barrier and seek support without diagnosing themselves or promising that one seating change will always work."], "question": "What should Riley bring to the follow-up?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Describe the sounds, lost time, and the change that helped, then ask for a future plan.", "consequence": "Specific observations help Ms. Chen understand the request. They discuss seating and who can help review ongoing needs. Riley leaves with a next step and a date to check back, not a promise that every test will be easy."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask a counselor or trusted staff member to join the planning conversation.", "consequence": "Riley does not have to navigate every school process alone. The staff member helps them prepare questions and identify the right person for support. Riley remains part of the conversation rather than having others decide everything for them."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Demand an automatic perfect score because the room was noisy.", "consequence": "Ms. Chen cannot agree to a score that does not reflect the work. Riley redirects toward a fair review of the conditions and available ways to demonstrate learning. A reasonable request can be firm without choosing the outcome in advance."}]}, {"title": "A clearer starting point", "text": ["The test is over, but the useful part is not just the grade. Riley can now name what made the work difficult, what helped, and who to contact before the next assessment. The follow-up may take more than one conversation.", "Riley writes one sentence to use next time: ‘I want to show what I know, and this is the barrier I am running into.’ Asking early is worth practicing. Asking late is still better than deciding their needs do not count."]}]}, {"id": "more-school-rumor", "title": "The Answer Everyone Thinks They Know", "category": "School drama", "teaser": "A rumor turns a good grade into an accusation. Facts matter more than winning the group chat.", "skill": "Disputing an accusation and seeking a fair process", "artCategory": "School drama", "scenes": [{"title": "A notification after lunch", "text": ["Maren opens the class chat and sees a message: ‘Funny how somebody suddenly got the highest score.’ Someone has attached a cropped photo showing Maren looking sideways during yesterday's quiz. The photo does not show that the teacher had been speaking from that side of the room.", "Maren feels furious. They worked for that grade, and now people are treating a guess as proof. The teacher has not said there is a problem. Maren wants the rumor stopped, but a long public fight could give it a larger audience."]}, {"title": "Before hitting send", "text": ["Maren types a reply naming every person who has ever asked to copy homework, then stops. Exposing other people will not explain what happened in the photo. They can preserve the message without forwarding it to more classmates.", "A friend asks whether Maren is okay. This is a chance to choose a response that protects their own position rather than turning the afternoon into a contest of accusations."], "question": "What is a useful first move?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Save the relevant message and contact the teacher privately with a factual explanation.", "consequence": "Maren keeps the original context and asks whether any concern has been raised. The teacher can respond to actual information rather than screenshots stripped of context. Maren has not agreed with the rumor by choosing a private channel."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Post one brief factual correction, then stop debating and seek adult support.", "consequence": "Maren writes that the teacher was speaking beside them and that they will address concerns directly. Some classmates accept it; others keep commenting. Maren does not need unanimous approval before leaving the argument."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reply with insults and accusations about everyone else.", "consequence": "The chat fills with new claims, and the original issue gets harder to separate from the argument. Maren can stop, save the relevant exchange, and move to a factual conversation. Their own harsh reply does not make the original rumor true."}]}, {"title": "A conversation about evidence", "text": ["The teacher, Mr. Soto, says he has seen the message but has not reached a conclusion. He asks Maren to explain the moment and says he will review what he observed. Maren worries that being asked a question means the decision is already made.", "They want to defend themselves while understanding the process. A fair review should not depend on who speaks loudest or which student is most popular."], "question": "What should Maren ask?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask what information is being reviewed and when they can expect a response.", "consequence": "Mr. Soto explains the next steps and agrees on a time to follow up. Maren can now distinguish a question from a final decision. If the process remains unclear, they know what still needs clarification."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask to bring a trusted staff member to a follow-up conversation.", "consequence": "Mr. Soto agrees to arrange a meeting. Maren wants support keeping the facts clear, not someone to attack classmates. Preparing a short timeline helps them explain the situation without getting pulled into every comment."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Refuse to answer any questions because an innocent person should never have to explain.", "consequence": "The conversation stalls. Maren decides they can object to the rumor and still provide information: ‘I did not cheat. I want a fair review, and here is what happened.’ Answering does not require accepting an accusation as fact."}]}, {"title": "The rumor does not vanish", "text": ["Mr. Soto confirms that the photo alone does not establish cheating and says he will address inappropriate sharing. Maren's score remains as recorded. One classmate still makes a comment in the hallway. A formal clarification has not changed every person's opinion.", "Maren has a choice about the next encounter. They do not owe classmates private details from the meeting, and they do not have to tolerate repeated harassment alone."], "question": "How should Maren handle another comment?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "State a short boundary and leave the exchange.", "consequence": "‘The school reviewed it. Stop making accusations about me.’ Maren continues to class. A boundary is not a debate invitation. If comments continue, they can document the pattern and ask staff for help."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Tell a trusted staff member exactly what was said and request support.", "consequence": "Maren reports the words, time, and location without adding guesses about motives. Staff can help with a continuing problem. Choosing support is not proof that Maren cannot handle a disagreement; it is a practical response to repetition."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Try to force the classmate to apologize in front of everyone.", "consequence": "The classmate performs for the audience, and the tension rises. Maren steps away before the exchange gets bigger. They can seek accountability through school support without trying to control a public apology."}]}, {"title": "More than a rumor", "text": ["Maren still dislikes what happened. They do not have to call it harmless just because the score was unchanged. They have protected the facts, asked about the process, and identified what to do if the comments continue.", "Their next step is ordinary: get to class, finish the next assignment, and spend time with people who do not turn every uncertainty into entertainment. Self-advocacy can mean making a clear record and then refusing to give an argument the rest of your day."]}]}, {"id": "more-school-hallway", "title": "Not What I Meant", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "One hallway direction sounds like an accusation. There is still a chance to reset the conversation.", "skill": "Clarifying misunderstandings without escalating", "artCategory": "School drama", "scenes": [{"title": "Between bells", "text": ["Dante is walking back from the office with a signed note when Mr. Webb calls, ‘You need to move along.’ Dante hears the words as another assumption that he is avoiding class. He has already spent the morning explaining himself to people who were not listening.", "Mr. Webb is watching several students near a crowded doorway. Dante does not know whether the comment was directed only at him. He wants to say he is already going where he belongs, but the reply forming in his head is sharper than that."]}, {"title": "The first words back", "text": ["Dante stops. Two students glance over, which makes the moment feel bigger. He can explain his destination without giving everyone the details of his office visit. He can also ask whether the instruction was meant for him.", "The hallway is noisy. If either person is misunderstanding the other, speaking louder may not make the meaning clearer."], "question": "How should Dante respond first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Say where he is headed and show the note without arguing about motives.", "consequence": "‘I'm returning to class from the office. I have a note.’ Mr. Webb sees that Dante has a reason to be there. The explanation creates a starting point, even if Dante still dislikes the tone of the first comment."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask, ‘Were you speaking to me? I'm on my way back to class.’", "consequence": "Mr. Webb says he was asking the group to clear the doorway. Dante realizes the instruction was not necessarily an accusation. Checking meaning prevents him from building a whole argument around an assumption."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Say, ‘You always have something to say about me.’", "consequence": "Mr. Webb focuses on Dante's tone, and the practical information gets lost. Dante can interrupt the pattern: ‘Let me explain that differently. I'm returning from the office, and I thought you meant I was doing something wrong.’"}]}, {"title": "A feeling that remains", "text": ["The direction is clearer now, but Dante is still irritated. He says he felt singled out. Mr. Webb replies that he was trying to keep the doorway open. Neither explanation automatically cancels the other person's experience.", "Dante wants the adult to understand without turning this into a hallway hearing. He also needs to get back before he misses more class. The conversation may need a better time and place."], "question": "What could Dante do next?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask to discuss the concern privately later and continue to class.", "consequence": "Mr. Webb agrees to a brief conversation after lunch. Dante has not dropped the issue; he has chosen a setting with fewer spectators. He can prepare one example and one request instead of collecting every past frustration."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Explain one specific concern now, then ask what he should do next time.", "consequence": "Dante says, ‘I wasn't sure you saw my note. Next time should I show it as I walk through?’ Mr. Webb answers. A concrete question gives both people something they can actually do differently."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stay in the doorway until Mr. Webb admits he was wrong.", "consequence": "The doorway becomes more crowded, and the original instruction becomes relevant again. Dante decides to move first and request a later conversation. Making space does not mean he agrees with every word that was said."}]}, {"title": "A chance to repair", "text": ["Later, Dante meets Mr. Webb near an empty classroom. The rush is over. Dante wants to explain that being assumed guilty is a sore point, but he does not have to share his entire history to make a useful request.", "He also remembers his own words. If he used a sharp response, he can take responsibility for that part while still addressing what bothered him. Repair is not the same as declaring only one person mattered."], "question": "What should the follow-up sound like?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Name his reaction and request a clearer, quieter direction next time.", "consequence": "‘I came back sharply because I thought you were accusing me. I'd like to explain where I'm going without an audience.’ Mr. Webb agrees to be clearer when possible. Dante has expressed a preference rather than demanded perfect control over every future interaction."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Bring a counselor if he finds the conversation difficult to manage alone.", "consequence": "With support, Dante describes the event and listens to Mr. Webb's account. The counselor helps them settle on a simple way to check misunderstandings. Getting help is especially useful if this has become a repeated pattern."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Pretend nothing bothered him because talking about feelings seems weak.", "consequence": "The conversation ends quickly, but Dante's frustration stays unspoken. He can still write down the concern and request another chance. Naming an experience does not require a dramatic confession; a clear sentence can be enough."}]}, {"title": "A different route through conflict", "text": ["Dante returns to class with no guarantee that he will never feel misunderstood again. What he has is a sequence he can use: check what was meant, explain the relevant fact, move out of an escalating moment, and follow up if needed.", "He does not have to like every direction to decide how he wants to respond. The next time a comment lands badly, a few seconds of clarification may protect more of his day than getting the last word ever could."]}]}, {"id": "more-school-grade", "title": "One Bad Grade, One Next Step", "category": "Pressure & conflict", "teaser": "A failed assignment feels like proof that trying is pointless. The next conversation could change the plan.", "skill": "Handling setbacks and requesting useful feedback", "artCategory": "School drama", "scenes": [{"title": "The page face down", "text": ["Imani turns the marked assignment over before anyone nearby can see it. She spent two evenings working on it. The score is far below what she expected, and the comments seem to point at everything she did wrong without telling her where to start.", "Her first thought is to stop trying. If hard work still leads here, why bother? A friend asks how she did, and Imani says the teacher just does not like her. Saying it out loud makes the explanation feel certain, even though she has not asked about the feedback."]}, {"title": "Before throwing it away", "text": ["The teacher, Ms. Vale, announces office hours for questions. Imani does not want to sit through a lecture about effort. She needs to understand the gap between what she thought the assignment required and what the teacher expected.", "She is upset enough that a conversation right now might turn into an argument. She can choose a short pause without giving up on getting answers."], "question": "What should Imani do first?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Save the assignment, take a brief pause, and arrange a specific time to ask questions.", "consequence": "Imani puts the paper away rather than tearing it up. A scheduled conversation gives the pause an endpoint. She can return with questions once the first rush of frustration settles."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask immediately which part would make the biggest difference to improve.", "consequence": "Ms. Vale points to the missing evidence in the main paragraph. Imani now has a concrete starting point. She can ask for an example instead of trying to fix every comment at once."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tell everyone the class is pointless and stop submitting work.", "consequence": "Skipping the next task would add another problem without explaining this one. Imani catches herself before making that plan final. She can be angry and still choose to get information before deciding her next move."}]}, {"title": "Effort and results", "text": ["In the meeting, Imani explains how long she worked. Ms. Vale acknowledges the effort and shows how the grading guide separates an opinion from evidence supporting it. Imani understands one comment better, but she still wants her work recognized.", "They discuss whether revision is available and what the rules are. Imani needs an accurate answer, not a promise that every assignment can be redone or that effort automatically changes a score."], "question": "What should Imani ask next?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask for one example of stronger evidence and a chance to practice it.", "consequence": "They compare two short paragraphs. Imani identifies the difference and tries revising one sentence. The feedback becomes an action she can practice, not a label about her ability."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask what revision options exist and what deadline or limits apply.", "consequence": "Ms. Vale explains the available option for this assignment and the exact deadline. If revision were unavailable, Imani could still use the feedback on the next task. Knowing the rule helps her plan realistically."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Insist the score must change because she worked harder than other people.", "consequence": "Ms. Vale cannot grade effort by comparing students' private time. Imani changes the request: ‘Can you show me what my work is missing and what I can do next?’ Her effort matters, but she needs a usable route forward."}]}, {"title": "The next blank page", "text": ["The following assignment arrives before Imani feels confident again. She recognizes the same skill and worries about repeating the mistake. A friend says they should both skip it. That would avoid a difficult evening, but not the problem waiting afterward.", "Imani does not need a perfect comeback story. She needs a plan small enough to begin and clear enough to check."], "question": "What is a workable next step?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Draft one paragraph early and ask for feedback on the evidence before the deadline.", "consequence": "A short early check catches the same gap before it spreads through the whole assignment. Imani still does the work herself, but she uses support when it can help rather than after every decision is already made."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Break the assignment into short sections and arrange a study session with a reliable peer.", "consequence": "They agree to explain the task to each other, not copy answers. The structure makes starting less overwhelming. Imani writes down one question she still needs to ask Ms. Vale."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Wait until the last night because starting means risking another failure.", "consequence": "The delay briefly lowers the anxiety, then adds time pressure. Imani can still begin with one section and ask what support remains available. A late start is not a reason to abandon every useful next step."}]}, {"title": "Progress without a guarantee", "text": ["Imani cannot guarantee the next grade. She can see a difference in how she is approaching the work: clearer questions, a smaller first step, and feedback she understands. One disappointing score has become information rather than a complete verdict on her future.", "She writes a reminder at the top of her planning page: ‘I can be frustrated and still ask what comes next.’ The goal is not to never feel discouraged. It is to keep a feeling from making every decision for her."]}]}, {"id": "more-school-friend", "title": "I Care, and I Am at Capacity", "category": "Friendships", "teaser": "Your friend needs to talk. You care about them, but you are running out of room yourself.", "skill": "Setting caring boundaries and connecting support", "artCategory": "Friendships", "scenes": [{"title": "Another long message", "text": ["Tessa's phone lights up while she is finishing homework and helping prepare dinner. Her friend Luca has had an argument at home and wants to talk through every detail. Tessa cares, but this is the fourth evening this week that their conversation has lasted past midnight.", "She has been sleeping badly and falling behind. She feels guilty admitting that answering the phone now makes her stomach tighten. Luca's message says, ‘You're the only one who understands.’ Tessa wants to be a friend without becoming the only support Luca is allowed to have."]}, {"title": "A reply with a limit", "text": ["Tessa starts typing ‘I'm busy’ and worries it will sound cold. She can acknowledge Luca's feelings without promising unlimited time. She also does not need to explain every private responsibility to justify a boundary.", "There is no mention of immediate danger in the message. If Luca later says someone is unsafe, that would call for trusted adult or urgent support rather than a promise to keep everything between friends."], "question": "How should Tessa reply?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Offer a specific short time to talk and say what she can manage tonight.", "consequence": "‘I care, and I can talk for ten minutes after dinner. Then I need to finish work and sleep.’ Luca knows what is available. Tessa has offered connection without creating a commitment she cannot keep."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Say she cannot talk tonight and suggest another trusted person Luca could contact.", "consequence": "Luca is disappointed, but the reply is clear and caring. Tessa is not responsible for replacing every other support. Suggesting a trusted adult or another supportive person widens Luca's options rather than abandoning them."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Agree to stay up as long as Luca needs, despite knowing she cannot manage it.", "consequence": "Tessa feels resentful before the call begins. She can correct the promise: ‘I answered too quickly. I can listen briefly, but I cannot stay up tonight.’ An honest correction is kinder than disappearing halfway through an unlimited commitment."}]}, {"title": "The guilt message", "text": ["Luca replies, ‘Never mind. I guess I'm too much.’ Tessa feels pulled toward proving that she cares by dropping everything. She also knows this pattern leaves both of them upset: Luca depends on an unlimited promise, and Tessa cannot sustain it.", "A disappointed response does not automatically mean Tessa's limit was wrong. She can acknowledge the feeling without bargaining away every boundary."], "question": "What should Tessa say now?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Reassure Luca while keeping the same limit.", "consequence": "‘You're not too much as a person. I just don't have unlimited energy tonight. My ten-minute offer still stands.’ Tessa separates care for Luca from the amount of time available. Luca may still be disappointed, but the message is consistent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ask whether Luca wants help identifying another support for tonight.", "consequence": "Tessa offers to help think of a trusted adult or friend who may be available. She does not share Luca's private story with others without considering safety and permission. Support can be shared rather than resting on one exhausted person."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Accuse Luca of being dramatic and block them in anger.", "consequence": "The insult adds hurt to a conversation about limits. Tessa can pause notifications without insulting Luca, then return with a clear message when calm. If she needs distance, she can state it directly instead of turning exhaustion into a personal attack."}]}, {"title": "A healthier pattern", "text": ["The next day, Tessa and Luca talk when neither is in the middle of the previous night's argument. Tessa wants their friendship to include ordinary fun, not only emergencies and late-night debriefs. Luca says they did not realize how tired Tessa had become.", "They cannot solve everything at home in this conversation. They can agree on a pattern that respects both people and makes room for other support."], "question": "What agreement could help?", "choices": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ask each other whether there is time and energy before starting a heavy conversation.", "consequence": "They agree that ‘not right now’ is a real answer, not a test of loyalty. Tessa can offer another time when she actually has one. Luca can make a wider support plan rather than guessing whether a friend is available."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Plan regular short check-ins and also do something enjoyable together.", "consequence": "They schedule a brief walk and decide not every meeting has to be a full problem-solving session. Predictable connection can reduce uncertainty, while other adults or services address needs beyond what friendship can carry."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Promise never to say no again so Luca will not feel rejected.", "consequence": "The promise removes the very boundary they needed to discuss. Tessa revises it: ‘I can promise to be honest about what I can offer.’ A friendship has a better chance when neither person must pretend to have endless capacity."}]}, {"title": "Care that can last", "text": ["Tessa still cares about Luca's difficult days. She also turns her phone off at bedtime without treating rest as a betrayal. Luca begins speaking with another trusted person, and the friends make plans that are not built around a crisis.", "A caring boundary can sound like two truths at once: ‘You matter to me, and this is what I can manage.’ If someone is in immediate danger, getting appropriate help matters more than protecting a promise of secrecy. Ordinary friendship does not have to mean handling everything alone."]}]}];
